window.REVISION_SUBJECTS.push({"config":{"brandIcon":"☸️","brandSubtitle":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062)","brandTitle":"Buddhism (AQA RS A)","breadcrumb":"Religious Studies / AQA A / Buddhism","examBoard":"AQA GCSE","footerNote":"Original practice questions written to the published AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062) specification for Buddhism. They are not AQA past-paper questions, and the model answers are indicative exemplars, not official AQA mark schemes. Other valid answers will be credited in the exam.","heroEyebrow":"Component 1 · Buddhism · Beliefs \u0026 teachings · Practices","markingSchemaId":"aqa-rs-generic","pageTitle":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A: Buddhism Revision","papers":[{"id":"Beliefs","label":"Beliefs \u0026 teachings","subtitle":"Dhamma · the Buddha · Four Noble Truths (1.1–1.10)","title":"Buddhism: Key beliefs and teachings"},{"id":"Practices","label":"Practices","subtitle":"Worship, festivals and ethics (2.1–2.8)","title":"Buddhism: Practices"}],"storageId":"aqa_rs_a_buddhism","subjectArea":"Religious Studies","subjectName":"Buddhism"},"questions":{"paper1":[{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-1-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The concept of Dhamma (Dharma)","question":"Which one of the following is one of the Three Refuges (Three Jewels) in Buddhism?\nA) Nibbana\nB) Dhamma\nC) Karuna\nD) Samsara","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Dhamma. The Three Refuges are the Buddha, the Dhamma (his teaching) and the Sangha (the community). Nibbana is the goal, karuna is compassion and samsara is the cycle of rebirth, so none of these is a refuge.","evidence":["Three Refuges: Buddha, Dhamma, Sangha","\"Dhammam saranam gacchami\""],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"three refuges"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-1-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The concept of Dhamma (Dharma)","question":"Which one of the following best describes the Tipitaka?\nA) A Buddhist monastery\nB) A type of meditation\nC) The Pali Canon of scriptures\nD) A Buddhist festival","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C) The Pali Canon of scriptures. The Tipitaka ('three baskets') is the collection of scriptures used especially by Theravada Buddhists, made up of the Vinaya, Sutta and Abhidhamma Pitakas.","evidence":["Tipitaka: Vinaya, Sutta and Abhidhamma Pitakas"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"scriptures"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-1-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The concept of Dhamma (Dharma)","question":"Give two meanings of the word 'Dhamma' (Dharma) in Buddhism.","marks":2,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of:\n- The teaching of the Buddha.\n- Universal truth or natural law (the way things really are).\n- The path or practice that leads to the end of suffering.\n- (In Buddhist philosophy) the basic elements or events that make up experience.","evidence":["Dhamma as teaching and as universal law"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"meaning of dhamma"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-1-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The concept of Dhamma (Dharma)","question":"Name the three 'baskets' of the Pali Canon (Tipitaka).","marks":2,"command":"Name","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Credit any two for 2 marks:\n- Vinaya Pitaka (rules for monks and nuns).\n- Sutta Pitaka (the discourses or sermons of the Buddha).\n- Abhidhamma Pitaka (philosophical and psychological analysis of the teaching).","evidence":["Vinaya, Sutta, Abhidhamma"],"examTip":"Only two correct answers are needed for 2 marks even though there are three baskets; don't waste time explaining them.","answerTopic":"scriptures"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-1-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The concept of Dhamma (Dharma)","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in the Dhamma influences Buddhists today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Buddhists take refuge in the Dhamma as one of the Three Jewels. Many recite the refuges daily or at the start of puja ('Dhammam saranam gacchami'), so the Dhamma becomes the guide they turn to in deciding how to live, rather than following a god or their own desires.\n\n2. Buddhists study and listen to the Dhamma. For example, lay Buddhists attend Dhamma talks given by monks at a vihara or join study groups, and some chant suttas from memory. This helps them understand teachings such as the Four Noble Truths and put them into practice in their daily lives, for instance by keeping the Five Precepts.","evidence":["Three Refuges","Dhamma talks at viharas"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"influence of dhamma"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-1-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The concept of Dhamma (Dharma)","question":"Explain two different ways in which Buddhists understand the authority of the Dhamma.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Many Buddhists see the Dhamma as truth to be tested, not believed blindly. The Buddha taught in the Kalama Sutta that people should accept a teaching only when they know for themselves that it leads to wellbeing, so Buddhists may try out meditation or the precepts and judge the results through experience.\n\n2. Other Buddhists place strong trust in particular texts or teachers. Theravada Buddhists treat the Pali Canon as the most reliable record of the Buddha's words, while many Mahayana Buddhists see sutras such as the Lotus Sutra as expressing the Buddha's highest teaching, and Tibetan Buddhists rely on a lama to pass on the Dhamma correctly.","evidence":["Kalama Sutta","Pali Canon","Lotus Sutra"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"authority of dhamma"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-1-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The concept of Dhamma (Dharma)","question":"Explain two Buddhist teachings about the Dhamma.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. The Dhamma is a tool to be used, not something to cling to. In the Parable of the Raft (Majjhima Nikaya 22), the Buddha compares his teaching to a raft that a man builds to cross a dangerous river; once across, he would be foolish to carry it on his head. This shows that the purpose of the Dhamma is practical: it helps people cross from suffering to nibbana.\n\n2. The Dhamma is a guide that replaces reliance on any single person. As he was dying, the Buddha told his followers to be 'a lamp unto yourselves' and to take the Dhamma as their refuge (Mahaparinibbana Sutta). This means Buddhists should follow the teaching and work out their own liberation rather than depend on the Buddha himself.","evidence":["Parable of the Raft (Majjhima Nikaya 22)","\"Be a lamp unto yourselves\" (Mahaparinibbana Sutta)"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"teachings about dhamma"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-1-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The concept of Dhamma (Dharma)","question":"Explain two reasons why the Dhamma is important to Buddhists.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. The Dhamma explains the cause of suffering and how to end it. In his first sermon at Sarnath, known as 'setting in motion the wheel of Dhamma' (Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta), the Buddha taught the Four Noble Truths. Without the Dhamma Buddhists would not know that craving causes suffering or that the Eightfold Path leads to nibbana.\n\n2. The Dhamma is one of the Three Refuges. Buddhists recite 'Dhammam saranam gacchami' ('I go for refuge to the Dhamma'). Like a shelter in a storm, the teaching gives security and direction in a world marked by impermanence, so it is central to Buddhist identity and worship.","evidence":["Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta","\"Dhammam saranam gacchami\""],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"importance of dhamma"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-1-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The concept of Dhamma (Dharma)","question":"\"The Dhamma is the most important of the Three Refuges.\"\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nThe Dhamma could be seen as the most important refuge because it is the truth itself. Buddhists believe the Buddha did not create the Dhamma but discovered it: the laws of impermanence, dependent arising and kamma would be true even if no Buddha had taught them. If the Dhamma is the reality that leads to nibbana, then the other two refuges depend on it.\n\nThe Buddha himself seemed to point his followers towards the Dhamma rather than himself. In the Mahaparinibbana Sutta he told them to be 'a lamp unto yourselves' and to take the Dhamma as their refuge after his death. He also said that whoever sees the Dhamma sees him. This suggests that the teaching outlasts the teacher and is what really matters.\n\nThe Dhamma is also what the Sangha preserves and passes on. Monks and nuns memorise and chant the suttas and give Dhamma talks; without the teaching, the community would have no purpose. In this sense the Dhamma is the centre of the wheel around which the others turn.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nOn the other hand, many Buddhists would argue that the Three Refuges cannot be ranked because they depend on each other. Without the Buddha, no one would have discovered and taught the Dhamma in this age; without the Sangha, the Dhamma would not have been preserved for 2,500 years. Taking refuge in all three together is what makes a person a Buddhist.\n\nSome Buddhists place great emphasis on the Buddha. In Pure Land Buddhism, faith in Amitabha Buddha and reciting his name (the nembutsu) is the central practice, so the Buddha refuge seems most important. Devotion to the Buddha in puja, in front of a Buddha rupa, is also how many lay Buddhists express their faith.\n\nOthers stress the Sangha. The Buddha told Ananda that good friendship is 'the whole of the holy life', and in practice many people learn the Dhamma through teachers and fellow practitioners. The Parable of the Raft also warns against clinging even to the Dhamma, so perhaps it should not be treated as the 'most important' thing in a fixed way.\n\nCONCLUSION\nOverall, I think the statement is partly convincing. The Dhamma does have a special status because it is the truth that leads to the end of suffering, and the Buddha directed people to it. However, the Buddha and the Sangha are the means through which people meet and live the Dhamma, so most Buddhists would see the three refuges as a unity. The strongest conclusion is that the Dhamma is the content of the refuges, but it cannot be separated from the teacher who revealed it and the community that keeps it alive.","evidence":["\"Be a lamp unto yourselves\" (Mahaparinibbana Sutta)","Parable of the Raft","Pure Land: nembutsu","Three Refuges"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"three refuges"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-1-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","moduleTitle":"The concept of Dhamma (Dharma)","question":"\"Buddhists should only accept the Dhamma if they can test it for themselves.\"\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nThe Buddha himself encouraged people to test his teaching. In the Kalama Sutta, the Kalama people were confused by many teachers with competing ideas. The Buddha told them not to accept something because of tradition, scripture, logic or the authority of a teacher, but to accept it when they knew for themselves that it was skilful and led to welfare and happiness. This strongly supports the statement.\n\nMuch of the Dhamma can be tested in experience. A person can notice impermanence and dukkha in everyday life, or see through meditation how craving creates dissatisfaction. Many Western Buddhists, including those at mindfulness and meditation centres in Britain, value Buddhism precisely because it invites investigation rather than blind faith.\n\nThe Parable of the Raft also shows that the Dhamma is a practical tool. You judge a raft by whether it gets you across the river, so it makes sense to judge the Dhamma by whether it actually reduces suffering.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nHowever, some parts of the Dhamma cannot easily be tested by ordinary people. Teachings about rebirth, kamma working across lifetimes, or the nature of nibbana go beyond present experience. Most Buddhists accept these partly through saddha (faith or confidence) in the Buddha, who is believed to have seen them directly on the night of his Enlightenment.\n\nIn some traditions trust is central. Pure Land Buddhists rely on faith in Amitabha Buddha's vow rather than on their own efforts, and Shinran taught that ordinary people cannot achieve Enlightenment through their own power. Tibetan Buddhists place deep trust in their lama, believing an experienced teacher can see further than a beginner.\n\nAlso, the Kalama Sutta does not say that people should reject everything they cannot yet test. The Buddha's point was to examine teachings carefully and to rely on the advice of the wise, so a beginner might reasonably accept some teachings provisionally, like a student trusting a doctor before they understand medicine.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI think the statement is mostly right in spirit but too strong as written. The Buddha clearly wanted people to investigate his teaching and not follow it blindly, and much of it can be tested. However, Buddhist practice also involves confidence in the Buddha and trusted teachers, especially regarding teachings like rebirth that go beyond ordinary experience. A balanced view is that Buddhists should test what they can, while holding the rest with reasoned trust until their own understanding grows.","evidence":["Kalama Sutta","Parable of the Raft","Saddha (faith/confidence)","Pure Land: reliance on Amitabha"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"testing the dhamma"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-2-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The concept of dependent arising (paticcasamupada)","question":"Which one of the following is the Pali term for dependent arising?\nA) Paticcasamupada\nB) Parinibbana\nC) Panna\nD) Puja","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A) Paticcasamupada. This is the teaching that everything arises in dependence on conditions. Parinibbana is final nibbana at death, panna is wisdom and puja is devotional worship.","evidence":["Paticcasamupada = dependent arising"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"key terms"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-2-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The concept of dependent arising (paticcasamupada)","question":"In the Tibetan Wheel of Life, which animal at the centre represents ignorance?\nA) Snake\nB) Cockerel\nC) Pig\nD) Monkey","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C) Pig. The pig represents ignorance (delusion), the cockerel represents greed and the snake represents hatred. A monkey appears on the outer rim as a symbol of consciousness, not at the hub.","evidence":["Wheel of Life: pig, cockerel, snake"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"wheel of life"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-2-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The concept of dependent arising (paticcasamupada)","question":"Give two of the twelve links (nidanas) of dependent arising.","marks":2,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of: ignorance; mental formations; consciousness; name-and-form (mind and body); the six senses (sense bases); contact; feeling (sensation); craving (tanha); clinging (grasping); becoming; birth; ageing and death.","evidence":["The twelve nidanas"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"twelve links"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-2-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The concept of dependent arising (paticcasamupada)","question":"Give two features of the Tibetan Wheel of Life (bhavachakra).","marks":2,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of:\n- It is held by Yama/Mara, representing death and impermanence.\n- The hub shows a pig, cockerel and snake (the Three Poisons).\n- It shows the six realms of rebirth.\n- The outer rim shows the twelve links of dependent arising.\n- The Buddha stands outside pointing to the moon (nibbana).\n- A ring shows beings rising (good kamma) and falling (bad kamma).","evidence":["Bhavachakra imagery"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"wheel of life"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-2-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The concept of dependent arising (paticcasamupada)","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in dependent arising influences Buddhists today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Buddhists try to change the conditions that lead to suffering. Since craving arises from feeling, a Buddhist might practise mindfulness so that when an unpleasant or pleasant feeling arises they notice it without automatically reacting with craving or anger. This breaks the chain at a key link.\n\n2. Buddhists recognise their interconnection with other beings and the world. Because nothing exists independently, many Buddhists, following ideas like Thich Nhat Hanh's 'interbeing', take care of the environment, avoid waste and act compassionately, knowing that harming others or nature also harms the conditions for their own wellbeing.","evidence":["Twelve links: feeling and craving","Interbeing (Thich Nhat Hanh)"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-2-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The concept of dependent arising (paticcasamupada)","question":"Explain two ways in which dependent arising is linked to other Buddhist teachings.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Dependent arising explains impermanence (anicca). If everything depends on conditions that are constantly changing, then everything must also be constantly changing; nothing can remain the same for ever, which is why Buddhists teach that clinging to things brings suffering.\n\n2. Dependent arising is the basis of the Four Noble Truths. The second truth says suffering arises from a cause (craving), and the third says it ceases when the cause ceases. This is the same pattern as dependent arising: 'when this ceases, that ceases'. So the path to nibbana works by removing the conditions for suffering.","evidence":["Anicca","Four Noble Truths: samudaya and nirodha"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"links to other teachings"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-2-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The concept of dependent arising (paticcasamupada)","question":"Explain two Buddhist teachings about dependent arising.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Everything arises because of conditions. The Buddha taught: 'When this exists, that comes to be; with the arising of this, that arises. When this does not exist, that does not come to be; with the cessation of this, that ceases' (Samyutta Nikaya 12). This means nothing, including a person, exists independently; it is part of a web of causes.\n\n2. Understanding dependent arising is understanding the Dhamma itself. The Majjhima Nikaya (MN 28) says, 'Whoever sees dependent arising sees the Dhamma.' This shows that the teaching is not just one idea among many; it is the key insight the Buddha gained at Enlightenment, which explains both how suffering arises and how it can be ended.","evidence":["Samyutta Nikaya 12: \"When this exists, that comes to be…\"","MN 28: \"Whoever sees dependent arising sees the Dhamma\""],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"teachings"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-2-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The concept of dependent arising (paticcasamupada)","question":"Explain two ways in which the Tibetan Wheel of Life illustrates Buddhist teaching about dependent arising.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. The hub shows the Three Poisons as the conditions which keep the wheel turning. The pig (ignorance), cockerel (greed) and snake (hatred) bite each other's tails, showing they feed one another. This illustrates the teaching that ignorance, the first of the twelve nidanas, conditions everything else in samsara.\n\n2. The outer rim shows the twelve links as a chain of pictures, such as a blind person for ignorance and a person drinking for craving. As the Buddha taught, 'with the arising of this, that arises' (Samyutta Nikaya 12), so each image depends on the one before. The Buddha pointing to the moon outside the wheel shows that by breaking the chain one can reach nibbana.","evidence":["Bhavachakra","Samyutta Nikaya 12"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"wheel of life"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-2-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The concept of dependent arising (paticcasamupada)","question":"“Dependent arising is the most important Buddhist teaching.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nDependent arising can be seen as the most important teaching because it was central to the Buddha's Enlightenment. Buddhist texts describe the Buddha contemplating the twelve links forwards and backwards on the night he became enlightened. The Majjhima Nikaya says, 'Whoever sees dependent arising sees the Dhamma; whoever sees the Dhamma sees dependent arising.' This suggests that understanding paticcasamupada is the same as understanding Buddhism.\n\nIt also underpins many other teachings. Impermanence (anicca) follows from it, because conditions keep changing. No-self (anatta) follows from it, because a person is a process depending on the five aggregates rather than a fixed soul. Even the Four Noble Truths have the same structure: suffering has a cause, and when the cause ends, suffering ends. Without dependent arising, these teachings would lack their explanation.\n\nIn Mahayana Buddhism it is linked to sunyata (emptiness), which the Heart Sutra presents as the highest wisdom. Thinkers such as Nagarjuna taught that emptiness and dependent arising are the same truth, which makes it central for Mahayana as well.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nHowever, many Buddhists would say the Four Noble Truths are the most important teaching. They were the subject of the Buddha's first sermon at Sarnath and give a clear practical diagnosis and cure for suffering, including the Eightfold Path. Dependent arising explains why, but the Four Noble Truths tell Buddhists what to do.\n\nFor many lay Buddhists, the most important teachings in daily life are ethical ones such as the Five Precepts, kamma, metta and karuna. A Buddhist may never study the twelve links in detail but still live a good Buddhist life through generosity and kindness.\n\nIn Pure Land Buddhism, the key teaching is Amitabha Buddha's vow to bring all who call on his name to the Pure Land. For these Buddhists, faith and the nembutsu matter more than understanding complex philosophy.\n\nIt could also be argued that teachings cannot be ranked because the Dhamma is a whole, like the spokes of a wheel which all need each other.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI think dependent arising has a strong claim to be the most important teaching in terms of understanding, because it explains the logic behind anicca, anatta and the Four Noble Truths. However, in terms of practice, the Four Noble Truths and the Eightfold Path are more directly useful, and different traditions put the emphasis elsewhere. So the statement is convincing philosophically, but less convincing as a description of how all Buddhists actually live.","evidence":["MN 28","Heart Sutra / Nagarjuna: emptiness","Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta (first sermon)","Pure Land: Amitabha's vow"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"importance"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-2-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","moduleTitle":"The concept of dependent arising (paticcasamupada)","question":"“Understanding dependent arising helps Buddhists to live better lives.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nDependent arising teaches that suffering has causes which can be changed. If a Buddhist understands that craving arises from feeling, they can practise mindfulness to stop pleasant and unpleasant feelings turning into craving and aversion. This is a practical way of reducing anger, jealousy and anxiety, which makes life better for the person and those around them.\n\nIt encourages responsibility. Because every action is a condition for future results, Buddhists see that their choices shape their character and future (linked to kamma). The Buddha taught, 'with the arising of this, that arises', so harmful actions lead to harmful results and skilful actions to good ones. This motivates people to keep the precepts.\n\nIt also encourages compassion and care for the world. Thich Nhat Hanh's idea of interbeing teaches that we 'inter-are' with other people, animals and the environment. Buddhists inspired by this have campaigned on climate change and supported peace work, seeing that harming others ultimately harms everyone.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nOn the other hand, the teaching can be very abstract. The twelve links are complex and their meaning is debated (for example whether they describe three lifetimes or each moment of experience). Simply understanding the theory does not automatically change behaviour; a person could understand it intellectually and still act selfishly.\n\nSome Buddhists stress that what really improves life is practice: meditation, keeping the precepts, generosity and devotion. Many devoted lay Buddhists in Sri Lanka or Thailand live very compassionate lives without studying paticcasamupada in detail.\n\nA non-religious person might argue that people can live good lives without any Buddhist teaching at all. Ideas about cause and effect and interconnection are found in science and in secular ethics; you do not need dependent arising to realise that pollution harms people or that anger damages relationships.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI think the statement is largely true, provided 'understanding' means more than knowing a list. Dependent arising gives a clear reason to change the causes of suffering, to take responsibility and to care for others, and the Buddha presented it as the heart of the Dhamma. But it helps only when it is put into practice through mindfulness and ethics, and good lives are possible in other ways too. So understanding dependent arising can help Buddhists live better lives, but it is not enough on its own.","evidence":["Samyutta Nikaya 12","Interbeing (Thich Nhat Hanh)","Twelve links: feeling and craving","Kamma"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-3-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"The Three Marks of Existence","question":"Which one of the following is the Buddhist term for 'no fixed self'?\nA) Anicca\nB) Anatta\nC) Dukkha\nD) Tanha","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Anatta. Anatta means there is no permanent, unchanging self or soul. Anicca is impermanence, dukkha is suffering/unsatisfactoriness and tanha is craving.","evidence":["Anatta = no fixed self"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"key terms"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-3-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"The Three Marks of Existence","question":"Which one of the following is NOT one of the Three Marks of Existence?\nA) Anicca\nB) Dukkha\nC) Anatta\nD) Karuna","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"D) Karuna. Karuna means compassion, an ethical quality. The three marks are anicca (impermanence), dukkha (suffering) and anatta (no fixed self).","evidence":["Tilakkhana"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"three marks"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-3-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"The Three Marks of Existence","question":"Name two of the Three Marks of Existence.","marks":2,"command":"Name","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of: anicca (impermanence); anatta (no fixed self); dukkha (suffering/unsatisfactoriness).","evidence":["Tilakkhana"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"three marks"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-3-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"The Three Marks of Existence","question":"Give two examples that Buddhists might use to illustrate anicca (impermanence).","marks":2,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two, e.g.:\n- The human body ageing from baby to old age.\n- A Tibetan sand mandala being swept away.\n- Seasons changing / leaves falling.\n- A candle flame that is never exactly the same from moment to moment.\n- Feelings and thoughts arising and passing in meditation.\n- Kisa Gotami learning that every family has experienced death.","evidence":["Sand mandala","Kisa Gotami"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"anicca"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-3-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"The Three Marks of Existence","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in anicca (impermanence) influences Buddhists today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Buddhists try not to become attached to possessions, people or situations. Because everything changes, clinging to things as if they will last brings suffering, so a Buddhist might enjoy good things while accepting they will end, and live simply rather than chasing material wealth.\n\n2. Belief in impermanence helps Buddhists cope with loss and death. A Buddhist who is bereaved may reflect, as Kisa Gotami learned, that death comes to every family. This does not remove grief, but it can bring acceptance and a calmer response, and it encourages people to value and be kind to others while they can.","evidence":["Kisa Gotami","Non-attachment"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"anicca"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-3-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"The Three Marks of Existence","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in anatta influences Buddhists today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Belief that there is no fixed self helps Buddhists reduce selfishness and pride. If 'I' is just a changing collection of the five aggregates, there is less reason to put one's own desires first, so Buddhists try to act generously and compassionately towards others.\n\n2. Anatta means people are able to change. Because there is no fixed self, a person is not trapped by their past character; a Buddhist who struggles with anger, for example, can work on it through metta meditation and the precepts, believing that transformation and ultimately Enlightenment are possible.","evidence":["Five aggregates","Metta meditation"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"anatta"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-3-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"The Three Marks of Existence","question":"Explain two Buddhist teachings about anatta (no fixed self).\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. A person is made of parts, with no separate self beyond them. In the Milindapanha, the monk Nagasena explains to King Milinda that a chariot is not its wheels, axle or frame, nor anything apart from them; 'chariot' is just a name for the parts together. In the same way, a person is a name for the five aggregates working together.\n\n2. Seeing that all things are not-self leads away from suffering. The Dhammapada (279) says, 'All things are not-self. When one sees this with wisdom, one turns away from suffering.' Craving and clinging come from believing in a fixed 'me' that must be protected and satisfied, so realising anatta reduces craving and moves a person towards nibbana.","evidence":["Milindapanha: the chariot","Dhammapada 279"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"anatta"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-3-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"The Three Marks of Existence","question":"Explain two ways in which the Three Marks of Existence are connected.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Impermanence explains no fixed self. Because everything is constantly changing (anicca), there cannot be an unchanging soul at the centre of a person. The Buddha taught in the Anattalakkhana Sutta that each of the five aggregates is impermanent, and whatever is impermanent cannot be a permanent self, so anatta follows from anicca.\n\n2. Impermanence and no-self lead to dukkha when people cling. The Dhammapada (277–278) teaches that 'all conditioned things are impermanent' and 'all conditioned things are unsatisfactory'. People suffer because they crave lasting pleasure and a permanent identity in a world where nothing lasts; seeing the marks with wisdom is the way to turn away from suffering.","evidence":["Anattalakkhana Sutta","Dhammapada 277–279"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"three marks"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-3-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"The Three Marks of Existence","question":"“Anatta is the most difficult Buddhist teaching to accept.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nAnatta goes against a very strong human instinct. Most people feel that there is a real 'me' that stays the same throughout life, from childhood to old age. Saying that there is no fixed self can seem to contradict everyday experience, so many people find it hard to accept.\n\nIt also seems to conflict with ideas about rebirth and kamma. If there is no self, it is natural to ask who is reborn and who receives the results of actions. Buddhists answer with the image of one candle lighting another, or with the chariot analogy in the Milindapanha, but the answer is subtle and many find it confusing.\n\nMany religions, such as Christianity, Islam and Hinduism, teach that people have an eternal soul (in Hinduism, the atman). Someone coming from these backgrounds may find anatta especially challenging, as it denies something central to their worldview and to hopes of life after death.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nOn the other hand, anatta can be supported by experience and reasoning. In meditation, Buddhists observe thoughts, feelings and perceptions arising and passing, and they cannot find any permanent 'thing' behind them. Modern psychology and neuroscience also describe the self as a changing process rather than a fixed soul, which makes anatta easier to accept for many people today.\n\nOther teachings might be harder to accept. Rebirth across many lifetimes cannot be tested in ordinary experience, and the idea that nibbana is beyond description can seem very mysterious. Some people find the teaching that 'life is dukkha' harder, because it seems pessimistic when they feel happy.\n\nAnatta can also be freeing rather than difficult. If there is no fixed self, people can change and grow, and there is less reason for pride or selfishness. Some Mahayana Buddhists also speak of Buddha-nature, which gives a positive picture alongside anatta.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI think anatta is probably the most difficult teaching to understand fully, because it challenges our deepest sense of who we are and raises puzzles about rebirth. However, 'difficult to accept' is different from 'difficult to understand'. For many Buddhists, especially through meditation and modern understanding of the mind, anatta becomes convincing, while rebirth and nibbana remain matters of trust. So the statement is reasonable, but not true for every Buddhist.","evidence":["Milindapanha: the chariot","Candle flame analogy for rebirth","Atman (Hindu soul)","Buddha-nature"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"anatta"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-3-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","moduleTitle":"The Three Marks of Existence","question":"“Believing that life is dukkha (suffering) is too negative.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nAt first the teaching that life is dukkha can sound pessimistic. Many people experience a lot of joy in life, through friends, family, achievements and beauty, so saying that all conditioned things are unsatisfactory might seem to ignore the good parts of life.\n\nFocusing on suffering, old age and death could make people anxious or depressed, especially young people. A non-religious humanist might say we should focus on making the most of our one life and that happiness is possible here and now, without labelling life as suffering.\n\nSome critics also argue that teaching dukkha might encourage people to withdraw from the world rather than enjoy it or improve it, as when the Buddha himself left his family and palace life.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nBuddhists respond that teaching dukkha is realistic, not negative. The Buddha was like a doctor diagnosing an illness: he identified the problem (dukkha) in order to cure it. The Four Noble Truths go on to say that suffering has a cause and can be ended, and the Eightfold Path shows how. This is a very hopeful message.\n\nDukkha does not mean that there is no happiness. It means that happiness based on impermanent things cannot last, so even pleasant experiences carry the seed of loss. Recognising this helps Buddhists enjoy things without clinging, which can actually lead to a deeper and more stable contentment.\n\nThe teaching also leads to compassion. Recognising that everyone suffers encourages Buddhists to practise karuna and metta. Engaged Buddhists, such as those inspired by Thich Nhat Hanh, work to reduce suffering through peace work and social action. The Buddha himself was described as smiling and joyful, and Buddhist practice aims at peace and happiness, not misery.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI disagree with the statement. Although dukkha might sound negative on its own, in Buddhism it is the first step of a process that leads to freedom from suffering. Like a doctor's diagnosis, it is honest and necessary. Because Buddhism teaches that suffering can end and offers a practical path, believing life is dukkha is ultimately realistic and hopeful rather than too negative.","evidence":["Four Noble Truths","Buddha as doctor analogy","Dhammapada 278","Engaged Buddhism"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"dukkha"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-4-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The human personality in the Theravada and Mahayana traditions","question":"Which one of the following is one of the Five Aggregates (skandhas)?\nA) Compassion\nB) Perception\nC) Generosity\nD) Patience","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Perception. The five aggregates are form, sensation, perception, mental formations and consciousness. Compassion is karuna, and generosity and patience are among the six perfections.","evidence":["Five aggregates"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"five aggregates"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-4-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The human personality in the Theravada and Mahayana traditions","question":"Which one of the following best describes sunyata?\nA) Loving kindness\nB) Emptiness\nC) Rebirth\nD) Impermanence","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Emptiness. Sunyata is the Mahayana teaching that all things are empty of fixed, independent existence. Loving kindness is metta, rebirth is part of samsara and impermanence is anicca.","evidence":["Sunyata = emptiness"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"sunyata"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-4-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The human personality in the Theravada and Mahayana traditions","question":"Name two of the Five Aggregates (skandhas).","marks":2,"command":"Name","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of: form (rupa); sensation/feeling (vedana); perception (sanna); mental formations (sankhara); consciousness (vinnana).","evidence":["Five aggregates"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"five aggregates"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-4-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The human personality in the Theravada and Mahayana traditions","question":"Give two Mahayana teachings about the human personality.","marks":2,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of:\n- All things, including the aggregates, are empty (sunyata).\n- All beings have Buddha-nature.\n- All beings have the possibility of attaining Buddhahood.\n- There is no fixed self; a person exists dependently.","evidence":["Sunyata","Buddha-nature"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"mahayana"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-4-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The human personality in the Theravada and Mahayana traditions","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs about the human personality in Theravada and Mahayana Buddhism.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Theravada Buddhists explain the human personality by analysing it into the five aggregates: form, sensation, perception, mental formations and consciousness. A person is simply these changing parts working together, and by seeing this clearly in vipassana meditation one can let go of the idea of a self and become an arhat.\n\n2. Many Mahayana Buddhists emphasise that every person has Buddha-nature, an inner potential to become a fully enlightened Buddha which is already present though hidden by greed, hatred and ignorance. This gives a more positive picture of human nature and supports the goal of becoming a Buddha, not just an arhat.","evidence":["Five aggregates","Buddha-nature"],"examTip":"In a 'contrasting' question, make sure your two points genuinely differ, for example one Theravada and one Mahayana belief. Two Theravada points would score less.","answerTopic":"theravada vs mahayana"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-4-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The human personality in the Theravada and Mahayana traditions","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in Buddha-nature influences Buddhists today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Buddhists who believe in Buddha-nature treat every person with respect, because everyone has the potential to become a Buddha. This might lead them to help people others reject, for example through Buddhist prison chaplaincy, believing even criminals can transform.\n\n2. Belief in Buddha-nature gives Buddhists confidence in their own practice. In Zen, for instance, practitioners sit in zazen to realise their own true nature rather than to gain something from outside. Believing Enlightenment is already within them encourages them to persevere in meditation even when it is difficult.","evidence":["Buddha-nature","Zazen","Angulimala"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"buddha-nature"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-4-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The human personality in the Theravada and Mahayana traditions","question":"Explain two Buddhist teachings about the Five Aggregates (skandhas).\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. None of the aggregates is a permanent self. In the Anattalakkhana Sutta, the Buddha asks the monks whether form, feeling, perception, formations and consciousness are permanent or impermanent. They answer 'impermanent', and he explains that what is impermanent cannot be called 'this is mine, this is my self'. So a person has no fixed soul.\n\n2. The aggregates come together like the parts of a chariot. In the Milindapanha, Nagasena explains that a chariot is just the name for wheels, axle, frame and so on assembled together. In the same way, a 'person' is a name given to the five aggregates together; there is no extra self hiding behind them.","evidence":["Anattalakkhana Sutta","Milindapanha: the chariot"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"five aggregates"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-4-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The human personality in the Theravada and Mahayana traditions","question":"Explain two Mahayana teachings about sunyata (emptiness).\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. All things are empty of independent existence. The Heart Sutra teaches, 'Form is emptiness, emptiness is form', meaning that even physical things (and the other aggregates) do not exist by themselves but arise through causes and conditions. Seeing this removes the basis for clinging and attachment.\n\n2. Emptiness is a middle way between extremes. The philosopher Nagarjuna taught that emptiness is the same as dependent arising: things are not permanently real, but they do not simply not exist either. This helps Mahayana Buddhists avoid both attachment to things and the despair of thinking nothing matters, and it supports the bodhisattva's wise compassion.","evidence":["Heart Sutra: \"Form is emptiness, emptiness is form\"","Nagarjuna"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"sunyata"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-4-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The human personality in the Theravada and Mahayana traditions","question":"“The Theravada teaching about the human personality is more convincing than the Mahayana teaching.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nThe Theravada teaching of the five aggregates is clear and can be tested. In vipassana meditation a person can observe for themselves bodily sensations, feelings, perceptions, thoughts and awareness arising and passing. This fits the Buddha's encouragement in the Kalama Sutta to know things for oneself.\n\nIt is based on what Theravada Buddhists see as the earliest record of the Buddha's teaching, the Pali Canon. In the Anattalakkhana Sutta, the Buddha's second sermon, he explains anatta by going through each aggregate. Theravada Buddhists may argue this has a stronger claim to be the Buddha's own words than later Mahayana sutras.\n\nThe five aggregates also fit well with modern psychology, which describes the mind as a set of changing processes rather than a single fixed soul. Someone might find this more convincing than talk of a hidden Buddha-nature, which might sound like a soul by another name.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nMahayana Buddhists would argue that their teaching builds on and completes the Theravada view rather than contradicting it. The Heart Sutra itself begins with the five aggregates, but says they are empty. Sunyata extends anatta to all things, which follows logically from dependent arising, so it could be seen as more thorough.\n\nBuddha-nature gives a more hopeful and motivating picture of humanity. If all beings have the potential to become Buddhas, then everyone deserves respect, and no one is beyond change. This supports the bodhisattva ideal of compassion for all beings and may be more inspiring than analysis of the aggregates.\n\nConvincing is also partly a matter of experience. Zen practitioners report that in zazen they glimpse their true nature, and Tibetan teachers speak of the natural clarity of mind. For them the Mahayana teaching describes their experience better.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI think neither teaching is simply more convincing, because they answer slightly different questions. The Theravada five aggregates are a careful analysis of what a person is made of, and are easier to test. The Mahayana teachings explain what a person can become and how all beings are connected. Since many Mahayana Buddhists accept the five aggregates too, the strongest view is that the Mahayana teaching adds to the Theravada one, and a person might find the combination most convincing overall.","evidence":["Anattalakkhana Sutta","Heart Sutra","Kalama Sutta","Buddha-nature"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"theravada vs mahayana"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-4-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","moduleTitle":"The human personality in the Theravada and Mahayana traditions","question":"“Everyone has the potential to become a Buddha.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nMany Mahayana Buddhists believe that all sentient beings have Buddha-nature. The Mahayana Mahaparinirvana Sutra teaches that all beings possess Buddha-nature, and the Lotus Sutra teaches that the Buddha's different teachings are skilful means leading everyone eventually to Buddhahood. On this view, the potential is universal.\n\nThe life of the Buddha supports this. Siddhartha was a human being, not a god, and he achieved Enlightenment through his own effort. If he could do it, others can follow the same path. The Buddha taught that even the most wicked can change: Angulimala, a murderer, became an arhat.\n\nAnatta also suggests everyone can change. If there is no fixed self, no one is permanently stuck with greed, hatred or ignorance. With practice of the Eightfold Path, these can be removed.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nTheravada Buddhists generally teach that the goal for most people is to become an arhat, not a Buddha. A fully enlightened Buddha (samma-sambuddha) is someone who rediscovers the Dhamma when it has been forgotten, and such Buddhas are extremely rare. So, though all may eventually reach nibbana, it is not usual to speak of everyone becoming a Buddha.\n\nIn practice, it may take countless lifetimes. Bodhisattvas are described as working for aeons before becoming Buddhas. For most people this potential is so distant that it hardly seems meaningful in this life.\n\nSome Pure Land Buddhists believe that in this 'degenerate age' (mappo) people cannot reach Enlightenment by their own power and must rely on Amitabha Buddha to be reborn in the Pure Land, where Enlightenment is easier. This suggests the potential exists but cannot be realised by ordinary effort alone.\n\nA non-religious person might reject the idea entirely, arguing that there is no evidence of rebirth or of Enlightenment as a real state.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI think the statement is true for most Mahayana Buddhists, who believe Buddha-nature is present in all beings, and it is consistent with the Buddha's own example and with anatta. However, Theravada Buddhists would usually say the realistic goal is to become an arhat, and many traditions stress how long and difficult the path is. So the statement is best described as true in principle in Mahayana thought, but not a belief all Buddhists share in the same way.","evidence":["Lotus Sutra","Mahayana Mahaparinirvana Sutra: Buddha-nature","Angulimala","Mappo (Pure Land)"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"buddhahood"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-5-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Human destiny: the Arhat and Bodhisattva ideals, Buddhahood and the Pure Land","question":"Which one of the following is the name of the Buddha at the centre of Pure Land Buddhism?\nA) Maitreya\nB) Amitabha\nC) Avalokiteshvara\nD) Manjushri","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Amitabha (Amida). Pure Land Buddhists hope to be reborn in Amitabha's Pure Land, Sukhavati. Maitreya is the future Buddha, and Avalokiteshvara and Manjushri are bodhisattvas.","evidence":["Amitabha / Sukhavati"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"pure land"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-5-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Human destiny: the Arhat and Bodhisattva ideals, Buddhahood and the Pure Land","question":"Which one of the following is the ideal goal in Theravada Buddhism?\nA) Bodhisattva\nB) Arhat\nC) Lama\nD) Rupa","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Arhat. An arhat is a 'perfected person' who has achieved nibbana and will not be reborn. The bodhisattva is the Mahayana ideal, a lama is a Tibetan teacher and a rupa is an image of the Buddha.","evidence":["Arhat"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"arhat"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-5-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Human destiny: the Arhat and Bodhisattva ideals, Buddhahood and the Pure Land","question":"Give two features of an arhat.","marks":2,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of:\n- They have achieved nibbana/Enlightenment.\n- They have destroyed greed, hatred and ignorance (the Three Poisons).\n- They will not be reborn after death.\n- They are calm, peaceful and free from fear.\n- They have followed the teaching of a Buddha (e.g. Sariputta).","evidence":["Dhammapada ch. 7"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"arhat"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-5-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Human destiny: the Arhat and Bodhisattva ideals, Buddhahood and the Pure Land","question":"Name two bodhisattvas.","marks":2,"command":"Name","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of: Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin/Chenrezig/Kannon); Manjushri; Tara; Maitreya; Kshitigarbha (Jizo); Samantabhadra; Vajrapani. (Also credit the Buddha in his previous lives, e.g. as Sumedha, or the Dalai Lama as an emanation of Avalokiteshvara.)","evidence":["Celestial bodhisattvas"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"bodhisattva"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-5-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Human destiny: the Arhat and Bodhisattva ideals, Buddhahood and the Pure Land","question":"Explain two contrasting beliefs about human destiny in Theravada and Mahayana Buddhism.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Theravada Buddhists believe the goal is to become an arhat, a perfected person who has removed greed, hatred and ignorance and reached nibbana, so they will not be reborn. This is usually achieved by following the teaching of the Buddha, often through monastic life and meditation.\n\n2. Mahayana Buddhists believe the highest goal is to become a Buddha by following the bodhisattva path. A bodhisattva vows to work for the Enlightenment of all beings, practising the six perfections over many lifetimes rather than seeking only their own liberation; some Mahayana Buddhists hope to be reborn in Amitabha's Pure Land where Enlightenment is easier.","evidence":["Arhat","Bodhisattva vow"],"examTip":"Make the contrast explicit, e.g. 'In contrast, Mahayana Buddhists…'. Each point should be developed with what the goal involves.","answerTopic":"theravada vs mahayana"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-5-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Human destiny: the Arhat and Bodhisattva ideals, Buddhahood and the Pure Land","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in the Pure Land influences Buddhists today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Pure Land Buddhists recite the nembutsu, 'Namu Amida Butsu', often many times a day or using a mala. This expresses gratitude and trust in Amida Buddha's vow and is their main religious practice, done at home and in temples, for example in Jodo Shinshu temples in Japan.\n\n2. Belief in the Pure Land gives comfort at death. Pure Land Buddhists may have images of Amida at a dying person's bedside and chant his name, believing that Amida will welcome them into Sukhavati. This brings hope and peace to the dying and their families.","evidence":["Nembutsu","Sukhavati"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"pure land"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-5-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Human destiny: the Arhat and Bodhisattva ideals, Buddhahood and the Pure Land","question":"Explain two Buddhist teachings about the bodhisattva ideal.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. A bodhisattva vows to help all beings. Shantideva writes: 'For as long as space endures, and for as long as living beings remain, until then may I too abide to dispel the misery of the world' (Bodhicaryavatara 10:55). This shows that the bodhisattva's motivation is compassion (karuna) for all beings, not just personal liberation.\n\n2. Bodhisattvas develop the six perfections (paramitas). The Perfection of Wisdom sutras teach that a bodhisattva must perfect generosity, morality, patience, energy, meditation and wisdom over many lifetimes. This shows that becoming a Buddha is a long path of training in both compassion and wisdom.","evidence":["Shantideva, Bodhicaryavatara 10:55","Perfection of Wisdom sutras: six paramitas"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"bodhisattva"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-5-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Human destiny: the Arhat and Bodhisattva ideals, Buddhahood and the Pure Land","question":"Explain two Buddhist teachings about the Pure Land.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Amitabha Buddha made a vow to save beings. According to the Larger Sukhavativyuha Sutra, as the monk Dharmakara he vowed that anyone who sincerely calls on his name with faith would be reborn in his Pure Land. This is why Pure Land Buddhists recite 'Namu Amida Butsu'.\n\n2. The Pure Land is a place where Enlightenment is easy to attain. The sutras describe Sukhavati as a beautiful land of jewelled trees and lotus ponds, free from suffering and distraction, where beings hear the Dhamma constantly. Shinran taught that ordinary people in this age cannot reach Enlightenment by their own power, so rebirth in the Pure Land through Amida's 'other-power' is the path for them.","evidence":["Larger Sukhavativyuha Sutra (Amitabha's vows)","Shinran: other-power (tariki)"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"pure land"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-5-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Human destiny: the Arhat and Bodhisattva ideals, Buddhahood and the Pure Land","question":"“Becoming a bodhisattva is a better ideal than becoming an arhat.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nMany Mahayana Buddhists argue that the bodhisattva ideal is better because it is motivated by compassion for all beings. A bodhisattva vows, 'Beings are numberless; I vow to save them all', and Shantideva prays to remain 'for as long as living beings remain'. This seems more selfless than seeking one's own nibbana.\n\nThe bodhisattva path aims at full Buddhahood, which includes perfect wisdom and compassion. The Lotus Sutra suggests that the arhat goal is a stage, a 'resting place', rather than the final destination, and that all are ultimately meant to become Buddhas. If Buddhahood is the highest state, then aiming for it seems the better ideal.\n\nThe bodhisattva ideal also inspires active social engagement. Many Mahayana Buddhists, such as followers of the Dalai Lama or members of Soka Gakkai and Fo Guang Shan, see helping others through education, charity and disaster relief as part of their path.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nTheravada Buddhists would argue that the arhat ideal is not selfish. Arhats have removed greed and hatred, and they naturally act with compassion. The Buddha's arhat disciples, like Sariputta, spent their lives teaching others. The Buddha told the first arhats to 'wander for the welfare of the many, out of compassion for the world'.\n\nThe arhat ideal is based on the Buddha's own teaching in the Pali Canon, which Theravada Buddhists see as the oldest record of his words. The Buddha was himself an arhat. Mahayana sutras that criticise the arhat goal came later and are not accepted by Theravada as the Buddha's words.\n\nThe arhat goal is also more achievable. The bodhisattva path takes countless lifetimes, while the arhat goal can be reached much sooner. Arguably, you cannot help others effectively until you have overcome your own greed, hatred and ignorance, just as a drowning person cannot rescue others.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI think the statement depends on which tradition you belong to. The bodhisattva ideal is impressive in its universal compassion, and for Mahayana Buddhists it is clearly the higher goal. However, it is unfair to call the arhat ideal selfish, since arhats are compassionate and teach others, and it has strong support in the earliest scriptures. Overall, both ideals combine wisdom and compassion; the bodhisattva ideal emphasises compassion more explicitly, but neither is simply 'better' for all Buddhists.","evidence":["Shantideva, Bodhicaryavatara","Lotus Sutra","\"Wander for the welfare of the many\" (Vinaya, Mahavagga)","Arhat Sariputta"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"arhat vs bodhisattva"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-5-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","moduleTitle":"Human destiny: the Arhat and Bodhisattva ideals, Buddhahood and the Pure Land","question":"“Pure Land Buddhists rely too much on Amitabha Buddha instead of their own efforts.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nThe Buddha's original teaching seems to stress self-effort. The Dhammapada says, 'You yourselves must strive; the Buddhas only point the way' (verse 276), and the Buddha told his followers to be 'a lamp unto yourselves'. Relying on Amitabha to bring you to the Pure Land might seem to go against this.\n\nTheravada Buddhists do not accept the Pure Land sutras as the Buddha's words, and they would see Enlightenment as something achieved through following the Eightfold Path, meditation and ethics, not by calling on a Buddha's name.\n\nSome critics argue that relying on another's power could make people passive, as if reciting the nembutsu is enough and moral effort is unnecessary. Even within Japanese Buddhism, Zen and Nichiren teachers criticised Pure Land for this reason.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nPure Land Buddhists believe relying on Amitabha is a realistic and humble response to human weakness. Shinran taught that in the degenerate age (mappo), ordinary people are too full of greed, hatred and ignorance to reach Enlightenment by themselves. Recognising this honestly and trusting Amida's compassionate vow is a way of letting go of the ego, which links to anatta.\n\nFaith in Amitabha does not mean doing nothing. Pure Land Buddhists still keep the precepts, practise generosity and recite the nembutsu with gratitude. Rebirth in the Pure Land is a means to Enlightenment, which they then hope to use to help others, like bodhisattvas.\n\nThe Pure Land path is open to everyone, not just monks with time for intensive meditation. Honen and Shinran wanted Buddhism to be accessible to ordinary farmers and workers. Also, all Buddhists rely to some extent on others: they take refuge in the Buddha, Dhamma and Sangha.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI do not think the statement is fair. From a Theravada view, relying on Amitabha may seem to neglect self-effort, and the Dhammapada does stress personal striving. But Pure Land Buddhists see trust in Amitabha as an honest recognition of human limitation and a way of overcoming self-centredness, and they still live ethical lives. 'Too much' assumes self-power is the only valid path, which Pure Land teaching rejects. So the statement reflects one tradition's view rather than a fair judgement on Pure Land Buddhism.","evidence":["Dhammapada 276","\"Be a lamp unto yourselves\"","Shinran: tariki and mappo","Nembutsu"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"pure land"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-6-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Buddha's life and its significance","question":"Which one of the following was NOT one of the Four Sights?\nA) An old man\nB) A sick man\nC) A newborn baby\nD) A holy man","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C) A newborn baby. The Four Sights were an old man, a sick man, a corpse (dead man) and a holy man (ascetic).","evidence":["The Four Sights"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"four sights"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-6-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Buddha's life and its significance","question":"Under which kind of tree did the Buddha gain Enlightenment?\nA) Oak tree\nB) Bodhi (pipal) tree\nC) Palm tree\nD) Cherry tree","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Bodhi (pipal) tree. Siddhattha sat beneath a pipal (fig) tree at Bodh Gaya, which became known as the Bodhi tree, the 'tree of awakening'.","evidence":["Bodh Gaya, Bodhi tree"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"enlightenment"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-6-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Buddha's life and its significance","question":"Give two of the Four Sights.","marks":2,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of: an old man (old age); a sick man (illness); a corpse (death); a holy man (wandering ascetic).","evidence":["Jataka 075 (cited in the AQA specification)"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"four sights"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-6-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Buddha's life and its significance","question":"Give two events from the Buddha's ascetic life.","marks":2,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of:\n- He studied meditation with teachers such as Alara Kalama and Uddaka Ramaputta.\n- He practised extreme fasting with five ascetics for about six years.\n- He became extremely thin and weak.\n- He accepted milk-rice from Sujata.\n- He realised extreme asceticism did not work and discovered the Middle Way.\n- The five ascetics left him.","evidence":["Ascetic life","Middle Way"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"ascetic life"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-6-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Buddha's life and its significance","question":"Explain two ways in which the Buddha's ascetic life influences Buddhists today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Buddhists follow the Middle Way. The Buddha learned that extreme self-denial weakened him as much as luxury distracted him, so Buddhists avoid extremes: monks and nuns live simply but eat enough to stay healthy, and lay Buddhists try to live without excess or harsh self-punishment.\n\n2. The Buddha's determination inspires Buddhists to persevere in meditation and practice. Even when his ascetic years failed, he kept searching for the truth. Buddhists today on retreat, or monks during the rains retreat (vassa), may follow his example of effort and discipline, while remembering that the aim is balance, not suffering.","evidence":["Middle Way","Lute string analogy"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"ascetic life"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-6-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Buddha's life and its significance","question":"Explain two ways in which the Four Sights are significant for Buddhists.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. The first three sights show the reality of dukkha and anicca. Siddhattha, who had been protected from suffering, discovered that ageing, illness and death come to everyone, even a prince. Buddhists see this as showing that no amount of wealth can escape suffering, which is the first Noble Truth.\n\n2. The fourth sight, the holy man, shows that there is a way to respond to suffering. The holy man was peaceful despite having nothing, which inspired Siddhattha to leave the palace and seek the truth. For Buddhists this shows that spiritual practice, rather than material comfort, can lead to peace, which encourages them to follow the path.","evidence":["Four Sights","Dukkha, anicca"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"four sights"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-6-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Buddha's life and its significance","question":"Explain two ways in which the Buddha's Enlightenment is significant for Buddhists.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. It shows that a human being can overcome suffering. The Buddha was not a god but became 'awakened' through his own effort. The Dhammapada (276) says, 'You yourselves must strive; the Buddhas only point the way.' This gives Buddhists hope that they too can reach Enlightenment by following his path.\n\n2. It is the source of the Dhamma. During the night of his Enlightenment the Buddha understood dependent arising and the Four Noble Truths, which he then taught in his first sermon at Sarnath (Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta). Without his Enlightenment, Buddhists would not have the teachings that guide their lives.","evidence":["Dhammapada 276","Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta","Bhumisparsha mudra"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"enlightenment"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-6-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Buddha's life and its significance","question":"Explain two ways in which the Buddha's birth and life of luxury are significant for Buddhists.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. The signs at his birth show he was a special being destined for greatness. According to the traditional accounts (e.g. the Nidanakatha and Buddhacarita), Queen Maya dreamed of a white elephant, and the sage Asita predicted he would be either a great king or a great spiritual teacher. Buddhists celebrate his birth at Wesak, honouring him as a uniquely important teacher.\n\n2. His life of luxury shows that pleasure and possessions cannot remove suffering. Despite having palaces, a wife and a son, Siddhattha was not truly satisfied. This teaches Buddhists that craving for material things leads to dukkha, as the second Noble Truth teaches, and encourages them not to seek happiness in wealth.","evidence":["Nidanakatha / Buddhacarita (birth accounts)","Second Noble Truth (tanha)"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"birth and luxury"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-6-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Buddha's life and its significance","question":"“The Four Sights were the most important event in the Buddha's life.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nThe Four Sights could be seen as the most important event because they were the turning point that started everything else. Before them, Siddhattha lived in luxury and knew nothing of suffering. Seeing old age, sickness and death showed him dukkha and anicca, and seeing the holy man showed him that there might be a way out. Without the sights, he might never have left the palace, so there would have been no ascetic life, no Enlightenment and no Buddhism.\n\nThe sights also contain key Buddhist teachings in simple form. They show the first Noble Truth (suffering is universal) and impermanence, which everyone can relate to. Buddhists today often reflect on them to remind themselves that they too will age, fall ill and die.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nMost Buddhists would argue that the Enlightenment was the most important event. This was when Siddhattha actually became the Buddha, 'the awakened one'. On that night he understood the Four Noble Truths and dependent arising. The Four Sights raised the question; the Enlightenment provided the answer. Bodh Gaya is the most important pilgrimage site for Buddhists, and many Buddha rupas show the earth-touching gesture from that night.\n\nOthers might argue the first sermon at Sarnath was most important, because it 'set the wheel of Dhamma in motion' and began the Sangha. Without the Buddha's decision to teach, his Enlightenment would not have helped anyone else.\n\nThe ascetic life was also crucial because it taught the Middle Way, a principle at the heart of Buddhist practice. And Mahayana Buddhists might see the Buddha's previous lives as a bodhisattva, told in the Jataka stories, as essential preparation.\n\nIt could also be argued that no single event is 'most important' because his whole life forms one story that teaches the Dhamma.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI think the Enlightenment was the most important event, because that is when Siddhattha became the Buddha and discovered the truths that Buddhists follow. The Four Sights were extremely significant as the trigger for his search and they powerfully illustrate dukkha, but they only raised the problem. So the statement is not convincing: the Four Sights were the most important starting point, but the Enlightenment was the most important event.","evidence":["Four Sights (Jataka 075)","Bodh Gaya / bhumisparsha mudra","Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta","Middle Way"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"significance of events"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-6-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","moduleTitle":"The Buddha's life and its significance","question":"“The Buddha's life is a perfect example for Buddhists today.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nFor Buddhists, the Buddha's life shows the whole path from ignorance to Enlightenment. He experienced luxury and extreme self-denial and found the Middle Way between them. This gives a practical, balanced model that is still relevant: avoid materialism, but also avoid harmful extremes.\n\nHis life shows that Enlightenment is possible for a human being through effort. He was not a god, so his example is something people can actually follow. The Dhammapada says 'the Buddhas only point the way', and his life is the clearest example of the way.\n\nAfter his Enlightenment he spent 45 years teaching anyone, regardless of caste or gender, including founding an order of nuns at Mahapajapati's request. His compassion and wisdom, and the calm way he faced death at Kushinagar, are qualities Buddhists today aim to develop.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nSome people criticise the Buddha for leaving his wife Yasodhara and baby son Rahula to follow his spiritual quest. Today, abandoning a family without support would be seen as irresponsible, so in this respect his life may not be a perfect model for lay Buddhists. Buddhists respond that he later returned and that Yasodhara and Rahula joined the Sangha, but the problem remains for many people.\n\nHis extreme ascetic period was something he himself said was a mistake, so not every part of his life is meant to be copied. He also lived in a very different society; few people today can become wandering renunciants.\n\nMany Buddhists also see the Buddha's life as unique. A fully enlightened Buddha who rediscovers the Dhamma is rare, so his role is to show the way rather than for everyone to copy his life exactly. Mahayana Buddhists may look also to bodhisattvas such as Avalokiteshvara as examples of compassion.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI think the Buddha's life is an excellent example of the Buddhist path, especially in showing the Middle Way, perseverance and compassion. However, it is not a perfect template to copy in every detail, because some episodes, like leaving his family and extreme fasting, are not meant to be imitated today, and his situation was unique. It is more accurate to say that his life shows the principles Buddhists should follow, rather than being a perfect example in every respect.","evidence":["Dhammapada 276","Middle Way","Yasodhara and Rahula","Mahapajapati and the nuns' order"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"the buddha as example"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-7-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: suffering (dukkha) and its different types","question":"Which one of the following is the first of the Four Noble Truths?\nA) Magga\nB) Nirodha\nC) Dukkha\nD) Samudaya","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C) Dukkha. The order is dukkha (suffering), samudaya (its cause), nirodha (its cessation) and magga (the path).","evidence":["Order of the Four Noble Truths"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"four noble truths"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-7-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: suffering (dukkha) and its different types","question":"Where did the Buddha preach his first sermon on the Four Noble Truths?\nA) Lumbini\nB) Sarnath\nC) Kushinagar\nD) Lhasa","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Sarnath. He taught the five ascetics at the Deer Park, Sarnath. Lumbini is his birthplace, Kushinagar where he died, and Lhasa is in Tibet.","evidence":["Deer Park, Sarnath"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"first sermon"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-7-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: suffering (dukkha) and its different types","question":"Name two types of suffering (dukkha) in Buddhist teaching.","marks":2,"command":"Name","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of: ordinary suffering/suffering of pain (dukkha-dukkha); suffering of change (viparinama-dukkha); suffering of conditioned existence/existential suffering (sankhara-dukkha). Also credit examples from the first sermon such as birth, ageing, illness, death, separation from what is loved.","evidence":["Three types of dukkha"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"types of suffering"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-7-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: suffering (dukkha) and its different types","question":"Give two examples of dukkha that the Buddha described in his first sermon.","marks":2,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of: birth; ageing; illness; death; sorrow; lamentation; pain; grief; despair; being with what is disliked; being separated from what is liked; not getting what one wants; the five aggregates subject to clinging.","evidence":["Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"dukkha"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-7-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: suffering (dukkha) and its different types","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in dukkha influences Buddhists today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Buddhists try to accept suffering calmly rather than deny it. Because dukkha is universal, a Buddhist facing illness or loss may reflect on the first Noble Truth, as Kisa Gotami learned, and respond with acceptance and mindfulness instead of despair or anger.\n\n2. Belief in dukkha motivates compassion. Recognising that all beings suffer, Buddhists practise karuna by helping those in need, for example volunteering in hospices, supporting charities or, for Mahayana Buddhists, following the bodhisattva ideal of working to relieve the suffering of all beings.","evidence":["Kisa Gotami","Karuna"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-7-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: suffering (dukkha) and its different types","question":"Explain two different types of suffering in Buddhist teaching.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Dukkha-dukkha is ordinary suffering, the physical and mental pain everyone recognises, such as a broken leg, illness, grief at a death or fear. It is the most obvious kind of suffering, illustrated by the first three of the Four Sights.\n\n2. Viparinama-dukkha is the suffering of change. Even pleasant experiences cause dissatisfaction because they are impermanent; for example, the joy of a holiday turns to sadness when it ends, or a person fears losing a relationship. This shows that because of anicca, even happiness is unsatisfactory if we cling to it.","evidence":["Dukkha-dukkha","Viparinama-dukkha","Anicca"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"types of suffering"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-7-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: suffering (dukkha) and its different types","question":"Explain two Buddhist teachings about dukkha.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Dukkha includes all the main experiences of life. In the Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta, the Buddha said, 'Birth is suffering, ageing is suffering, illness is suffering, death is suffering… In brief, the five aggregates subject to clinging are suffering.' This shows dukkha is not just occasional pain but something present throughout conditioned existence.\n\n2. Dukkha is to be understood, not ignored. The Buddha taught that each Noble Truth has a task, and the task of the first truth is to fully understand suffering. Like a doctor diagnosing an illness, this understanding is the first step towards a cure, which is why Buddhists see the teaching as hopeful rather than pessimistic.","evidence":["Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta","Doctor analogy"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"dukkha"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-7-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: suffering (dukkha) and its different types","question":"Explain two ways in which the story of Kisa Gotami illustrates Buddhist teaching about suffering.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. The story shows that suffering is universal. In the commentary on the Therigatha and the Dhammapada (on verse 114), Kisa Gotami asks the Buddha to bring her dead son back to life; he asks her to fetch a mustard seed from a house where no one has died. She finds every family has experienced death, which teaches the first Noble Truth that dukkha touches everyone.\n\n2. The story shows that understanding suffering leads to peace. Instead of being overwhelmed by grief, Kisa Gotami accepts impermanence (anicca), buries her son and joins the Sangha as a nun, later becoming an arhat. This shows that facing suffering honestly, rather than clinging, is the first step on the path to nibbana.","evidence":["Kisa Gotami (Therigatha / Dhammapada commentary)","Anicca"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"kisa gotami"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-7-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: suffering (dukkha) and its different types","question":"“Buddhism is right to teach that life is suffering.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nBuddhists argue that the teaching is simply realistic. Everyone experiences birth, ageing, illness and death, and no one can avoid loss, disappointment or not getting what they want. The Buddha's first sermon lists these, and the story of Kisa Gotami shows that no family escapes death. A teaching that faces these facts honestly seems right.\n\nThe teaching on the different types of suffering shows a deep understanding of human experience. It is not only pain (dukkha-dukkha) that causes dissatisfaction but also change (viparinama-dukkha): even our happiest moments end. This explains why many wealthy and successful people still feel unsatisfied.\n\nMost importantly, the Buddha taught dukkha as the first step towards a cure. Like a doctor, he named the illness so that it could be treated; the third and fourth Noble Truths show suffering can end. So the teaching is not just negative but leads to hope and practical change.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nSome people argue that 'life is suffering' is too negative and ignores the joy, love and beauty that many people experience. A humanist might argue that, although suffering exists, life can be deeply fulfilling and meaningful, and focusing on suffering could make people miserable or passive.\n\nOthers say the translation is misleading. Dukkha means 'unsatisfactoriness' rather than constant suffering, so 'life is suffering' is not exactly what the Buddha taught. Many Buddhists would agree with this criticism of the wording, and say life contains both happiness and dukkha.\n\nFollowers of other religions may agree that there is suffering but explain it differently. For example, Christians may see suffering as a result of sin and free will, and hope for an end to suffering through God rather than through ending craving.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI think Buddhism is right to teach that dukkha is part of all conditioned existence, because it fits human experience, especially impermanence and loss, and it leads to a practical response. However, the phrase 'life is suffering' oversimplifies the teaching, which accepts that happiness exists but says it cannot last or fully satisfy. So the statement is correct if understood as 'life is marked by dukkha', which is a realistic and ultimately hopeful view.","evidence":["Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta","Viparinama-dukkha","Kisa Gotami","Doctor analogy"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"dukkha"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-7-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: suffering (dukkha) and its different types","question":"“The suffering of change (viparinama-dukkha) is the worst kind of suffering.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nThe suffering of change could be seen as the worst because it affects even our happiest experiences. Physical pain is obvious and often temporary, but the knowledge that everything we love will change or be lost is always present. Even at a wedding or the birth of a child, there is the knowledge that these things will not last for ever.\n\nIt is linked directly to craving and clinging, which the Buddha taught is the cause of suffering. People often suffer most not from pain itself but from losing what they are attached to: a relationship, a job, their health or youth. Grief at the death of a loved one is perhaps the clearest example.\n\nBecause it is hidden in pleasure, it can trap people in samsara. People keep chasing new pleasures, not realising that each one will fade, so they never escape the cycle of craving.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nMany people would say ordinary suffering (dukkha-dukkha) is worse. Severe physical pain, starvation, war or serious illness can be overwhelming, and someone in great pain may not even be able to think about deeper forms of dukkha. Buddhists running hospices or relief work focus on this suffering because it is so urgent.\n\nBuddhist teaching might suggest that sankhara-dukkha, the suffering of conditioned existence, is the most fundamental. It is the subtle unsatisfactoriness of being a changing collection of aggregates with no fixed self. It underlies the other types and is only fully removed at Enlightenment, so it is the deepest problem.\n\nIt could also be argued that ranking suffering is not helpful. The Buddha taught that all dukkha comes from the same cause, tanha, so the aim is to remove craving, which will deal with all types together.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI think the suffering of change is very significant because it affects everyone, even in good times, and links clearly to craving and impermanence. However, ordinary suffering can be more intense in the moment, and in Buddhist terms the suffering of conditioned existence is the deepest. Overall, I do not agree it is the 'worst'; the most Buddhist answer is that all three types share one root in craving, so they must be dealt with together through the Eightfold Path.","evidence":["Three types of dukkha","Tanha","Anicca"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"types of suffering"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-8-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the causes of suffering (samudaya) and the Three Poisons","question":"Which one of the following is one of the Three Poisons?\nA) Hatred\nB) Compassion\nC) Wisdom\nD) Generosity","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A) Hatred. The Three Poisons are ignorance, greed and hatred. Compassion, wisdom and generosity are wholesome qualities that act as their antidotes.","evidence":["Three Poisons"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"three poisons"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-8-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the causes of suffering (samudaya) and the Three Poisons","question":"Which one of the following is the Pali word for craving?\nA) Tanha\nB) Sila\nC) Metta\nD) Magga","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A) Tanha. Tanha means craving or 'thirst' and is the cause of suffering in the second Noble Truth. Sila is ethics, metta is loving kindness and magga is the path.","evidence":["Tanha = craving/thirst"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"tanha"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-8-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the causes of suffering (samudaya) and the Three Poisons","question":"Name the Three Poisons.","marks":2,"command":"Name","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of: ignorance/delusion (moha); greed/attachment (lobha/raga); hatred/aversion (dosa).","evidence":["Three Poisons"],"examTip":"Only two correct answers are needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"three poisons"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-8-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the causes of suffering (samudaya) and the Three Poisons","question":"Give two types of craving (tanha) in Buddhist teaching.","marks":2,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of: craving for sense pleasures (kama-tanha); craving for existence/becoming (bhava-tanha); craving for non-existence/to get rid of things (vibhava-tanha).","evidence":["Kama-, bhava-, vibhava-tanha"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"tanha"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-8-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the causes of suffering (samudaya) and the Three Poisons","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in the Three Poisons influences Buddhists today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Buddhists try to replace greed with generosity (dana). Knowing that greed fuels suffering, a Buddhist might give food to monks, donate to charity or try to buy less, so that letting go of possessions weakens attachment.\n\n2. Buddhists try to overcome hatred through loving kindness. They may practise metta bhavana meditation, wishing well first to themselves and then to friends, strangers and even enemies, so that anger is replaced by goodwill. This affects how they respond to conflict in daily life, for example forgiving rather than retaliating.","evidence":["Dana","Metta bhavana"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-8-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the causes of suffering (samudaya) and the Three Poisons","question":"Explain two ways in which tanha (craving) causes suffering according to Buddhism.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Craving makes people cling to things that are impermanent. Because everything changes (anicca), whatever we crave will eventually be lost, so the more we cling to possessions, people or pleasures, the more we suffer when they change or end.\n\n2. Craving is never satisfied and keeps us in samsara. When one desire is fulfilled, another arises, like drinking salt water that makes you thirstier. The Buddha taught that this craving 'leads to renewed existence', creating kamma that causes rebirth and further suffering.","evidence":["Anicca","Craving leads to renewed existence"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"tanha"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-8-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the causes of suffering (samudaya) and the Three Poisons","question":"Explain two Buddhist teachings about the Three Poisons.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. The Three Poisons are like fires that burn. In the Fire Sermon (Adittapariyaya Sutta), the Buddha taught that 'everything is burning… with the fire of passion, with the fire of aversion, with the fire of delusion'. This shows greed, hatred and ignorance cause constant suffering, and nibbana means 'blowing out' these fires.\n\n2. The Three Poisons keep beings trapped in samsara. On the Tibetan Wheel of Life, a pig, cockerel and snake at the hub bite one another's tails, showing ignorance, greed and hatred feed each other and drive the cycle of rebirth. Ignorance is the root: because people do not see anicca and anatta, they crave and hate.","evidence":["Fire Sermon (SN 35.28)","Bhavachakra hub"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"three poisons"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-8-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the causes of suffering (samudaya) and the Three Poisons","question":"Explain two Buddhist teachings about craving (tanha).\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Craving is the cause of suffering. In the Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta, the Buddha said the origin of suffering is 'this craving which leads to renewed existence… seeking delight here and there'. This is the second Noble Truth, and it means that if craving can be removed, suffering will also end.\n\n2. Craving produces grief and fear. The Dhammapada (216) says, 'From craving springs grief, from craving springs fear; for one who is wholly free from craving there is no grief, much less fear.' This shows that the emotional pain people experience, such as worry about losing what they love, comes from attachment.","evidence":["Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta","Dhammapada 216","Dhammapada 251"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"tanha"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-8-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the causes of suffering (samudaya) and the Three Poisons","question":"“Greed is the main cause of suffering in the world today.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nGreed (lobha) is one of the Three Poisons, and the second Noble Truth identifies craving as the cause of suffering. Much suffering in today's world seems to come from greed: economic inequality, exploitation of workers, corruption and wars over resources. Buddhists would see these as greed on a large scale.\n\nConsumerism encourages kama-tanha, craving for sense pleasures. Advertising constantly tells people they need more possessions to be happy, which leads to debt, stress and dissatisfaction. Environmental destruction and climate change are also driven by the demand for more and more, causing suffering for humans and animals. The Dhammapada says there is 'no river like craving', suggesting its power to sweep people away.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nBuddhists teach that ignorance (moha), not greed, is the root poison. On the Wheel of Life, the pig of ignorance is often shown leading the others, and ignorance is the first of the twelve links of dependent arising. People are greedy because they do not understand that everything is impermanent and that there is no fixed self to satisfy. So ignorance may be the deeper cause.\n\nHatred (dosa) also causes enormous suffering, through violence, racism, terrorism and war. Many conflicts are driven by fear and hatred of others rather than greed alone. The three poisons work together, so it may be impossible to single out one as the main cause.\n\nMuch suffering also has natural causes, such as earthquakes, disease and ageing, which are not directly caused by anyone's greed. A non-religious view might stress poverty, lack of education or bad political systems as causes, rather than a spiritual problem of craving.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI think greed is a major cause of human-made suffering in the world today, especially through consumerism, inequality and environmental damage, and the Buddha's teaching on craving is very relevant. However, from a Buddhist perspective ignorance is the deeper root, and hatred and natural causes also play a big role. So greed is one of the main causes, but not 'the main cause' on its own; the three poisons must be understood together.","evidence":["Second Noble Truth","Dhammapada 251","Wheel of Life: pig, cockerel, snake","Twelve links: ignorance first"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"three poisons"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-8-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the causes of suffering (samudaya) and the Three Poisons","question":"“It is impossible to stop craving.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nCraving seems to be part of human nature. People naturally want food, comfort, love and security, and we seem to be biologically programmed to seek pleasure and avoid pain. Even very disciplined people still experience desires, so ending craving completely may seem impossible.\n\nModern society makes it even harder. Advertising, social media and consumer culture are designed to create new desires, encouraging kama-tanha and bhava-tanha (wanting status). For a lay person with a job, family and bills, living without any craving appears unrealistic.\n\nSome Buddhists themselves recognise how difficult it is. Pure Land Buddhists, following Shinran, believe that ordinary people in this age are full of blind passions and cannot end craving by their own power, so they rely on Amida Buddha.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nThe Buddha taught that craving can be stopped: this is the third Noble Truth (nirodha). He himself ended craving at his Enlightenment, and many disciples, including Kisa Gotami and Angulimala, became arhats. If it were impossible, the whole Buddhist path would be pointless.\n\nThe Eightfold Path offers practical methods. Mindfulness helps people notice desire arising without acting on it; metta and generosity weaken greed and hatred; wisdom about anicca and anatta removes the ignorance that feeds craving. Buddhists believe craving can gradually be reduced, even if complete ending takes a long time or many lifetimes.\n\nIt is also important to distinguish craving from all desire. Buddhists distinguish selfish craving from wholesome intention, such as the wish to become enlightened or to help others. Ending tanha does not mean becoming a robot with no wishes at all.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI disagree with the statement. Craving is very hard to stop and for most people it will not end quickly, especially in a consumer society. But Buddhism teaches, and claims to demonstrate through the Buddha and the arhats, that it can be ended through the Eightfold Path. So stopping craving is very difficult, but Buddhists would say it is not impossible, and even reducing it brings real benefits.","evidence":["Third Noble Truth (nirodha)","Arhats: Kisa Gotami, Angulimala","Shinran: blind passions","Eightfold Path"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"tanha"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-9-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the end of craving (nirodha), nibbana and Enlightenment","question":"What does the word 'nibbana' (nirvana) literally mean?\nA) Rebirth\nB) Blowing out\nC) Rising up\nD) Turning the wheel","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Blowing out. Nibbana means 'extinguishing', like a flame going out: the fires of greed, hatred and ignorance are blown out.","evidence":["Nibbana = 'blowing out'"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"nibbana"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-9-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the end of craving (nirodha), nibbana and Enlightenment","question":"Which one of the following is the third Noble Truth?\nA) Dukkha\nB) Samudaya\nC) Nirodha\nD) Magga","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C) Nirodha. The third truth is the cessation of suffering through the end of craving. Dukkha is the first, samudaya the second and magga the fourth.","evidence":["Order of the Four Noble Truths"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"four noble truths"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-9-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the end of craving (nirodha), nibbana and Enlightenment","question":"Give two ways in which Buddhists describe nibbana.","marks":2,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of: blowing out (of the fires of greed, hatred and ignorance); the end of suffering; the end of craving; the end of rebirth; the highest happiness/bliss; perfect peace; unconditioned/unborn; freedom; the further shore; the deathless.","evidence":["Dhammapada 204","Udana 8.3"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"nibbana"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-9-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the end of craving (nirodha), nibbana and Enlightenment","question":"Give two differences between nibbana in this life and parinibbana.","marks":2,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of:\n- Nibbana in this life is achieved while still alive (e.g. the Buddha at 35); parinibbana happens at death (the Buddha at about 80).\n- In this life the enlightened person still has a body and the five aggregates; at parinibbana they are not reborn.\n- In this life physical pain may still be felt; parinibbana is complete release from all conditioned existence.","evidence":["Parinibbana at Kushinagar"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"nibbana in life and death"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-9-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the end of craving (nirodha), nibbana and Enlightenment","question":"Explain two contrasting interpretations of nibbana (nirvana).","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Many Theravada Buddhists understand nibbana as the goal reached by an arhat through gradual practice of the Eightfold Path: the complete ending of craving, so that at death (parinibbana) they are not reborn. It is the unconditioned state beyond samsara.\n\n2. Many Mahayana Buddhists, following Nagarjuna, teach that nirvana and samsara are not ultimately separate: when seen with the wisdom of emptiness, nirvana is realised within the world itself. Zen Buddhists speak of awakening (satori) to one's Buddha-nature, sometimes suddenly, and bodhisattvas remain in the world out of compassion rather than leaving samsara.","evidence":["Arhat and parinibbana","Nagarjuna: samsara and nirvana","Satori"],"examTip":"For 'contrasting', show clearly how the two interpretations differ, using phrases such as 'whereas' or 'in contrast'.","answerTopic":"interpretations"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-9-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the end of craving (nirodha), nibbana and Enlightenment","question":"Explain two ways in which belief in nibbana influences Buddhists today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Belief that suffering can end gives Buddhists hope and motivation to follow the Eightfold Path. A Buddhist may meditate regularly and keep the precepts, believing that by reducing greed, hatred and ignorance they are moving closer to nibbana, even if it takes many lifetimes.\n\n2. Some Buddhists commit their whole lives to seeking nibbana by becoming monks or nuns. For example, Theravada monks in Britain at monasteries like Amaravati give up possessions and family life to dedicate themselves to meditation and study, while lay Buddhists support them with food (dana) to make merit.","evidence":["Eightfold Path","Amaravati Buddhist Monastery"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-9-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the end of craving (nirodha), nibbana and Enlightenment","question":"Explain two Buddhist teachings about nibbana.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Nibbana is the highest happiness. The Dhammapada (204) says 'Nibbana is the highest bliss'. This shows nibbana is not a negative state of nothingness but complete peace and freedom from suffering, which is why it is the goal of Buddhist practice.\n\n2. Nibbana is unconditioned. In the Udana (8.3) the Buddha says, 'There is an unborn, unbecome, unmade, unconditioned. If there were not, there would be no escape from the born, become, made and conditioned.' Everything in samsara arises from conditions and so is impermanent, but nibbana is beyond conditions and therefore offers real escape from suffering.","evidence":["Dhammapada 204","Udana 8.3"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"nibbana"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-9-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the end of craving (nirodha), nibbana and Enlightenment","question":"Explain two Buddhist teachings about what happens to an enlightened person after death.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. An enlightened person is not reborn. When the Buddha died at Kushinagar he entered parinibbana, final nibbana. The Pali Canon describes an arhat's realisation: 'Birth is ended… there is no more coming to any state of being.' This means the cycle of samsara has been broken because the craving that fuels rebirth has ended.\n\n2. It cannot be described as existing or not existing. In the Aggi-Vacchagotta Sutta (MN 72), the Buddha compared the question 'where does an enlightened person go?' to asking which direction a fire has gone when it goes out. The question does not apply. Buddhists therefore reject both the idea of an eternal soul in heaven and the idea of simple annihilation.","evidence":["Arhat formula: \"Birth is ended…\"","Aggi-Vacchagotta Sutta (MN 72)"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"parinibbana"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-9-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the end of craving (nirodha), nibbana and Enlightenment","question":"“Nibbana is impossible to understand.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nNibbana is described as beyond ordinary experience and language. The Udana calls it 'unborn, unbecome, unmade, unconditioned', and much of the language about it is negative: it is not a place, not a state of existence, not annihilation. If it can only be described by what it is not, it seems impossible to understand properly.\n\nThe Buddha refused to answer questions about what happens to an enlightened person after death. In the Aggi-Vacchagotta Sutta he said asking where an enlightened person goes is like asking where a fire goes when it goes out. If even the Buddha would not explain it in words, ordinary people cannot hope to understand it intellectually.\n\nDifferent traditions also interpret it differently. Theravada sees it as beyond samsara; Mahayana thinkers such as Nagarjuna say samsara and nirvana are not different; Zen speaks of sudden awakening; Pure Land focuses on rebirth in Sukhavati. These differences suggest that even Buddhists find it hard to pin down.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nBuddhists would say nibbana can be understood, but through experience rather than description. It is like trying to describe the taste of honey to someone who has never tasted it: words fail, but the taste is real and can be known. The Buddha and arhats such as Sariputta are said to have realised it directly.\n\nSome parts of nibbana are clearly understandable. It means the 'blowing out' of greed, hatred and ignorance, and the end of suffering. Most people can understand what it would be like to be completely free of anger and craving, even if only partly, as in moments of deep peace in meditation.\n\nThe Buddha taught the Eightfold Path as a practical method, and his teaching on nibbana was meant to motivate practice rather than satisfy curiosity. As the Parable of the Arrow (MN 63) suggests, a person shot by an arrow should remove it rather than ask who made it; similarly, Buddhists should work to end suffering instead of speculating.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI think nibbana is impossible to understand fully through words or reasoning alone, as the Buddha himself suggested. However, the statement goes too far. Its core meaning, the ending of craving and suffering, is clear, and Buddhists believe it can be understood directly through practice. So nibbana is difficult to describe, but not impossible to understand for someone who follows the path.","evidence":["Udana 8.3","Aggi-Vacchagotta Sutta (MN 72)","Parable of the Arrow (MN 63)","Nagarjuna"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"nibbana"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-9-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the end of craving (nirodha), nibbana and Enlightenment","question":"“Buddhists should aim for nibbana in this life rather than a good rebirth.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nThe Buddha taught that the goal of the path is nibbana, the end of suffering, not just a better position within samsara. Even a rebirth as a god in a heavenly realm is still impermanent and will end, so it is still dukkha. The Wheel of Life shows that beings who rise to the god realms can fall again. Aiming only for good rebirth could be seen as missing the point.\n\nThe Buddha himself and many of his disciples reached nibbana in a single life. The Dhammapada says 'you yourselves must strive', and Zen teaches that awakening can happen here and now by realising one's Buddha-nature. This suggests Buddhists should aim high now rather than postponing.\n\nCraving even a good rebirth could be a form of bhava-tanha (craving for existence), which the Buddha taught causes suffering.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nFor many lay Buddhists, especially in Theravada countries such as Thailand and Sri Lanka, reaching nibbana in this life is not realistic. They have jobs and families, and cannot meditate full-time like monks. Making merit through dana, keeping the precepts and supporting the Sangha to achieve a good rebirth is a sensible step on a path that may take many lifetimes.\n\nA good human rebirth is actually very valuable, because it is the best position from which to practise the Dhamma. The Buddha compared the rarity of a human birth to a blind turtle surfacing through a single yoke floating in the ocean. Aiming for such a rebirth is a way of aiming for nibbana in the long term.\n\nMahayana bodhisattvas deliberately choose to be reborn to help others, rather than entering final nirvana quickly, and Pure Land Buddhists aim for rebirth in Sukhavati as the way to Enlightenment. So aiming for a particular rebirth is not necessarily inferior.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI think nibbana is the true goal for all Buddhists, so in principle they should aim for it. However, aiming for nibbana and aiming for a good rebirth are not opposites: for most people a good rebirth is a step towards nibbana, and for bodhisattvas and Pure Land Buddhists rebirth serves the final goal. So the statement is right that nibbana is the ultimate aim, but wrong to suggest Buddhists who seek a good rebirth are misguided.","evidence":["Wheel of Life: six realms","Dhammapada 276","Blind turtle simile (SN 56.48)","Pure Land: Sukhavati"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"goal"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-10-0","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the Eightfold Path (magga) and the Threefold Way","question":"Which one of the following is part of the wisdom (panna) section of the Eightfold Path?\nA) Right speech\nB) Right intention\nC) Right effort\nD) Right livelihood","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Right intention. The wisdom section contains right understanding and right intention. Right speech and right livelihood belong to ethics (sila), and right effort belongs to meditation (samadhi).","evidence":["Threefold Way"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"threefold way"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-10-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the Eightfold Path (magga) and the Threefold Way","question":"Which symbol represents the Eightfold Path?\nA) A lotus with eight petals only\nB) A wheel with eight spokes\nC) A tree with eight branches\nD) A stupa with eight levels","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) A wheel with eight spokes. The dharmachakra's eight spokes represent the eight elements of the path, all needed for the wheel to turn.","evidence":["Dharmachakra"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"symbol"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-10-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the Eightfold Path (magga) and the Threefold Way","question":"Name the three sections of the Threefold Way.","marks":2,"command":"Name","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of: ethics/morality (sila); meditation/concentration (samadhi); wisdom (panna).","evidence":["Threefold Way"],"examTip":"Only two are needed for 2 marks. Pali terms are credited but English is fine.","answerTopic":"threefold way"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-10-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the Eightfold Path (magga) and the Threefold Way","question":"Give two parts of the Eightfold Path that belong to ethics (sila).","marks":2,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of: right speech (samma vaca); right action (samma kammanta); right livelihood (samma ajiva).","evidence":["Sila section"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"sila"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-10-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the Eightfold Path (magga) and the Threefold Way","question":"Explain two ways in which following the Eightfold Path influences Buddhists today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Right livelihood affects the jobs Buddhists choose. Because they should earn a living without causing harm, a Buddhist might avoid working in the arms trade, selling alcohol or in industries that harm animals, and might choose work that helps others, such as nursing or teaching.\n\n2. Right speech affects how Buddhists communicate. They try to avoid lying, gossip, harsh words and divisive speech, including online, and to speak truthfully and kindly. For example, a Buddhist might refuse to join in spreading rumours on social media, or speak up gently against bullying.","evidence":["Right livelihood","Right speech"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-10-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the Eightfold Path (magga) and the Threefold Way","question":"Explain two ways in which the three sections of the Threefold Way support each other.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Ethics (sila) supports meditation (samadhi). If a person lives ethically, for example by not lying or stealing, they will have less guilt, worry and conflict, so their mind is calmer and it is easier to concentrate in meditation.\n\n2. Meditation supports wisdom (panna), and wisdom supports ethics. Deep concentration and mindfulness allow a Buddhist to see impermanence and no-self directly, which brings wisdom. With wisdom they understand that harming others creates suffering, so they naturally behave more ethically, and the cycle continues, like the spokes of a wheel working together.","evidence":["Threefold Way","Dharmachakra"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"threefold way"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-10-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the Eightfold Path (magga) and the Threefold Way","question":"Explain two Buddhist teachings about the Eightfold Path.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. The Eightfold Path is the way to end suffering. The Dhammapada (190–191) says that one who takes refuge 'sees with right wisdom the Four Noble Truths… and the Noble Eightfold Path leading to the cessation of suffering'. This shows the path is the cure the Buddha prescribed for dukkha, the fourth Noble Truth.\n\n2. The Eightfold Path is the Middle Way. In the Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta, the Buddha told the five ascetics that he had avoided the two extremes of indulgence in sense pleasures and self-torment, and discovered the Middle Way, which is the Noble Eightfold Path. This teaches Buddhists to live a balanced life without extremes.","evidence":["Dhammapada 190–191","Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"eightfold path"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-10-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the Eightfold Path (magga) and the Threefold Way","question":"Explain two ways in which right mindfulness and right concentration are important for Buddhists.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Right mindfulness helps Buddhists see reality as it is. In the Satipatthana Sutta the Buddha teaches mindfulness of body, feelings, mind and mental objects. By noticing thoughts and feelings arising and passing, a Buddhist understands anicca and anatta directly, and can stop feelings turning into craving.\n\n2. Right concentration calms the mind so that insight can arise. The Dhammapada (372) says, 'There is no meditation without wisdom, no wisdom without meditation.' A focused, still mind is like calm water that reflects clearly, allowing the Buddhist to develop the wisdom which leads to nibbana.","evidence":["Satipatthana Sutta","Dhammapada 372"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"samadhi"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-10-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the Eightfold Path (magga) and the Threefold Way","question":"“Wisdom (panna) is the most important part of the Eightfold Path.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nWisdom could be seen as most important because ignorance is the root of suffering. Ignorance is the first of the twelve links of dependent arising and the pig at the centre of the Wheel of Life. Only wisdom, seeing anicca, anatta and dukkha directly, can remove it. Ethics and meditation alone could not end the cycle without this insight.\n\nRight understanding comes first in the list of the Eightfold Path because it gives the path its direction. Without understanding the Four Noble Truths, a person would not know why they are practising. Dhammapada 190–191 says that one who takes refuge 'sees with right wisdom' the Four Noble Truths, suggesting wisdom is central.\n\nIn Mahayana Buddhism, wisdom (prajna) is the sixth and culminating perfection, and the Perfection of Wisdom sutras such as the Heart Sutra are among the most important texts. Prajnaparamita is sometimes called 'the mother of all Buddhas'.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nMany Buddhists argue that ethics (sila) is the foundation. Without ethical behaviour, the mind is too disturbed for meditation, and so wisdom cannot develop. The Five Precepts are the starting point for lay Buddhists, and monks follow the Vinaya. For most lay Buddhists, living ethically is the most important part of the path in daily life.\n\nOthers would stress meditation (samadhi). Zen Buddhists see zazen as the heart of practice, and the Buddha's Enlightenment came through meditation under the Bodhi tree. The Dhammapada (372) says there is 'no wisdom without meditation'.\n\nThe strongest objection is that the path is a whole. It is symbolised by a wheel with eight spokes; if one spoke is missing the wheel will not turn. The three trainings support one another: ethics supports meditation, meditation produces wisdom, and wisdom leads to ethics.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI think wisdom has a special place because it is the only thing that directly removes ignorance, the root of suffering, and so leads to nibbana. However, it cannot be developed without ethics and meditation, and for many Buddhists ethics is the most practical starting point. So wisdom may be the goal of the path, but it is not 'most important' in a way that makes the other parts less necessary; all eight spokes are needed.","evidence":["Dhammapada 190–191","Dhammapada 372","Heart Sutra / Prajnaparamita","Dharmachakra"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"threefold way"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-10-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","moduleTitle":"The Four Noble Truths: the Eightfold Path (magga) and the Threefold Way","question":"“The Eightfold Path is too difficult for most Buddhists to follow today.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nThe Eightfold Path asks a lot. Right speech means never lying, gossiping or speaking harshly; right livelihood rules out many jobs; right mindfulness and concentration require regular, disciplined meditation. In a busy modern life with work, school, family and constant digital distraction, keeping all eight perfectly seems almost impossible.\n\nModern society often encourages the opposite of the path. Consumer culture encourages greed, social media can encourage harsh or untruthful speech, and many jobs involve some harm. A lay Buddhist might find it very hard to avoid every form of wrong livelihood in a global economy.\n\nTraditionally, full practice was mainly for monks and nuns, who give up family life to follow it completely. This suggests the full path is too demanding for ordinary lay people, and some Pure Land Buddhists say ordinary people today cannot succeed by their own effort.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nThe path is meant to be followed gradually, not perfectly from the start. Buddhists believe progress can be made over many lifetimes, and each small step reduces suffering. The Buddha called it the Middle Way: it is not about extreme effort but about balance.\n\nMany Buddhists today do follow it in practical ways. They meditate for a short time daily, keep the Five Precepts, choose ethical jobs and try to speak kindly. Organisations such as the Triratna Buddhist Community run ethical businesses based on right livelihood, and mindfulness practices have become popular even outside Buddhism, showing it can fit modern life.\n\nThe path can also make life easier, not harder. Ethical living reduces conflict and guilt, and meditation reduces stress. Many Buddhists find that the path helps them cope with the pressures of modern life rather than adding to them.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI disagree with the statement. Following the Eightfold Path perfectly is very difficult, and modern life brings particular challenges. But the path is designed to be practised gradually, and many lay Buddhists do follow it meaningfully today. Since even partial practice reduces suffering, it is not too difficult to follow; it is challenging, but that is what makes it a path of training rather than a list of rules.","evidence":["Middle Way","Right livelihood: Triratna businesses","Pure Land view of human weakness","Five Precepts"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"eightfold path"}],"paper2":[{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-1-0","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Buddhist places of worship: temples, shrines, viharas and gompas, Buddha rupa, artefacts and offerings","question":"Which one of the following is the name for a Buddhist monastery?\nA) Gurdwara\nB) Vihara\nC) Mandir\nD) Synagogue","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Vihara. A vihara is a Buddhist monastery where monks and nuns live. A gurdwara is Sikh, a mandir Hindu and a synagogue Jewish.","evidence":["Vihara"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"places of worship"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-1-1","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Buddhist places of worship: temples, shrines, viharas and gompas, Buddha rupa, artefacts and offerings","question":"What does the flower offered at a Buddhist shrine usually symbolise?\nA) Impermanence\nB) Rebirth in heaven\nC) The Sangha\nD) Wealth","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"A) Impermanence. Flowers are beautiful but soon wither, reminding Buddhists of anicca.","evidence":["Offerings: flowers and anicca"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"offerings"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-1-2","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Buddhist places of worship: temples, shrines, viharas and gompas, Buddha rupa, artefacts and offerings","question":"Give two offerings Buddhists might make at a shrine.","marks":2,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of: flowers; candles/lamps/light; incense; water (e.g. water bowls); food; fruit; money/donations; chanting or bowing (as devotional offerings).","evidence":["Shrine offerings"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"offerings"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-1-3","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Buddhist places of worship: temples, shrines, viharas and gompas, Buddha rupa, artefacts and offerings","question":"Give two features of a Buddha rupa.","marks":2,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of: ushnisha (bump on the head); urna (dot between the eyebrows); long ear lobes; half-closed eyes; a mudra (hand gesture) such as meditation, earth-touching or teaching; sitting in the lotus position; a calm, peaceful expression; a halo.","evidence":["Ushnisha, urna, mudras"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"buddha rupa"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-1-4","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Buddhist places of worship: temples, shrines, viharas and gompas, Buddha rupa, artefacts and offerings","question":"Explain two ways in which Buddhists use a vihara.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. A vihara is a home for monks and nuns, where they live according to the Vinaya, meditate and study the Dhamma. Their way of life is supported by the lay community, so the vihara keeps the Sangha alive.\n\n2. Lay Buddhists visit the vihara to make offerings and learn. For example, at Amaravati in Hertfordshire lay people bring food for the monks (dana), which makes merit, and they stay to listen to Dhamma talks, join group meditation or celebrate festivals such as Wesak.","evidence":["Amaravati","Dana","Vinaya"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"vihara"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-1-5","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Buddhist places of worship: temples, shrines, viharas and gompas, Buddha rupa, artefacts and offerings","question":"Explain two contrasting ways in which Buddhists might worship at a temple and at a home shrine.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. At a temple, worship is often communal and led by monks or nuns. Buddhists may chant together, listen to a Dhamma talk, meditate as a group in the shrine hall and offer food to the Sangha, which makes it a social occasion and builds community, especially at festivals.\n\n2. At a home shrine, worship is usually private and simpler. A Buddhist might light a candle and incense, bow three times to a small Buddha rupa and recite the Three Refuges or a mantra before meditating alone each morning. This fits worship into daily life without needing to travel.","evidence":["Shrine room","Home shrine"],"examTip":"For 'contrasting', make the difference clear, e.g. communal vs private, led vs individual.","answerTopic":"temple vs home"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-1-6","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Buddhist places of worship: temples, shrines, viharas and gompas, Buddha rupa, artefacts and offerings","question":"Explain two reasons why the Buddha rupa is important to Buddhists.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. The rupa reminds Buddhists of the Buddha's qualities and teaching. For example, the earth-touching mudra recalls the night of his Enlightenment, when he called the earth to witness against Mara. Seeing this encourages Buddhists to develop the same determination; as the Dhammapada (276) says, 'the Buddhas only point the way', and the rupa points to that way.\n\n2. The rupa is a focus for devotion and gratitude. Buddhists bow before it and recite 'Buddham saranam gacchami' ('I go for refuge to the Buddha'). It is not worshipped as a god, but showing respect to it expresses thanks for the Dhamma and helps develop humility and calm before meditation.","evidence":["Bhumisparsha mudra","Dhammapada 276","\"Buddham saranam gacchami\""],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"buddha rupa"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-1-7","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Buddhist places of worship: temples, shrines, viharas and gompas, Buddha rupa, artefacts and offerings","question":"Explain two reasons why Buddhists make offerings at a shrine.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Offerings remind Buddhists of key teachings. Flowers wither and remind Buddhists of impermanence; a traditional Pali verse chanted when offering flowers says that, just as these flowers will fade, so will this body. Candles symbolise the light of wisdom that dispels ignorance.\n\n2. Offerings develop generosity and make merit. Giving (dana) is the first of the six perfections and the antidote to greed, one of the Three Poisons. By giving freely without expecting anything back, Buddhists reduce attachment and create good kamma.","evidence":["Pali flower-offering verse","Dana","Three Poisons"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"offerings"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-1-8","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Buddhist places of worship: temples, shrines, viharas and gompas, Buddha rupa, artefacts and offerings","question":"“Buddhists do not need to go to a temple or vihara to practise their religion.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nThe heart of Buddhism is personal practice: keeping the precepts, meditation and developing wisdom. These can be done anywhere. The Buddha gained Enlightenment sitting under a tree, not in a temple, and he told his followers to be 'a lamp unto yourselves'.\n\nMany Buddhists have a shrine at home with a Buddha rupa, candles and incense, where they meditate, chant and make offerings every day. For them, the home shrine is as valid as any temple. Others practise using apps, online Dhamma talks and virtual sanghas, especially since the COVID-19 pandemic.\n\nIn Britain, temples can be far away; a Buddhist living in a rural area might find it impractical to visit regularly. What matters is the intention and practice, not the building. Zen Buddhists would say that zazen can be practised wherever one sits.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nThe Sangha is one of the Three Refuges, and the vihara is where the monastic Sangha lives. Lay Buddhists go there to support monks and nuns with dana, which is an important way of making merit, and to receive teaching. Without temples and viharas, the Sangha could not survive.\n\nTemples provide guidance from experienced teachers. Learning meditation alone can be difficult, and a monk, nun or lama can correct mistakes. Tibetan Buddhists believe receiving teachings and empowerments from a qualified lama is essential.\n\nTemples are also centres of community and festivals. Wesak, Parinirvana Day and Kathina are celebrated together, and the Buddha described spiritual friendship as 'the whole of the holy life'. Practising alone may lead to isolation or misunderstanding.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI agree that Buddhists do not strictly need to go to a temple, because the core of Buddhism is personal practice, which can be done at a home shrine or anywhere. However, temples and viharas are very valuable for supporting the Sangha, receiving teaching and building community, so most Buddhists benefit from going sometimes. The best view is that a temple is not essential, but it is an important support for practice.","evidence":["\"Be a lamp unto yourselves\"","Three Refuges: Sangha","Spiritual friendship (SN 45.2)","Home shrines"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"importance of temples"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-1-9","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","moduleTitle":"Buddhist places of worship: temples, shrines, viharas and gompas, Buddha rupa, artefacts and offerings","question":"“Offerings at a shrine are more important than meditation.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nFor many lay Buddhists, especially in countries such as Sri Lanka, Thailand and Myanmar, making offerings is a central part of daily religious life. Offering flowers, candles, incense and food is how they express devotion, gratitude and respect for the Buddha.\n\nOfferings develop generosity (dana), which is the first of the six perfections and the direct antidote to greed. Giving makes merit (good kamma) which leads to a better rebirth and helps support the Sangha. In Theravada Buddhism, giving food to monks is one of the most important acts a lay person can do.\n\nOfferings also teach. Wilting flowers remind Buddhists of impermanence, and light symbolises wisdom. So offerings are not just ritual; they help people reflect on the Dhamma, even those without time for long meditation.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nMeditation is part of the Eightfold Path itself (right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration), while making offerings is not explicitly listed. The Buddha reached Enlightenment through meditation, not through offerings, and meditation is how wisdom, which removes ignorance, develops.\n\nThe Buddha taught that the best way to honour him was by practising the Dhamma. In the Mahaparinibbana Sutta, when flowers rained down on him, he said that whoever practises the Dhamma honours him more than through such offerings. This suggests practice is more important than offerings.\n\nZen Buddhists see zazen as the heart of practice, and Western convert Buddhists often focus mainly on meditation, rarely making formal offerings. Offerings done without understanding could become empty ritual.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI think meditation is more important than offerings in terms of reaching nibbana, as the Buddha himself emphasised practising the Dhamma above outward honour. However, offerings are not unimportant: they develop generosity, express gratitude and prepare the mind for meditation, and for many lay Buddhists they are the most accessible part of practice. Ideally the two work together, with offerings as a support for meditation rather than a replacement.","evidence":["Mahaparinibbana Sutta (honouring through practice)","Dana","Eightfold Path: samadhi","Zazen"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"offerings vs meditation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-2-0","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Puja: devotional ritual in the home and temple, chanting, mantras and malas","question":"How many beads does a full Buddhist mala usually have?\nA) 8\nB) 27\nC) 108\nD) 1000","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C) 108. A full mala usually has 108 beads, used to count mantra recitations. Smaller wrist malas may have fewer (e.g. 27).","evidence":["Mala: 108 beads"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"mala"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-2-1","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Puja: devotional ritual in the home and temple, chanting, mantras and malas","question":"Which bodhisattva is the mantra 'Om mani padme hum' associated with?\nA) Manjushri\nB) Avalokiteshvara\nC) Maitreya\nD) Amitabha","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Avalokiteshvara, the bodhisattva of compassion. Manjushri is the bodhisattva of wisdom, Maitreya the future Buddha and Amitabha a Buddha (not a bodhisattva) linked to the Pure Land.","evidence":["Om mani padme hum"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"mantra"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-2-2","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Puja: devotional ritual in the home and temple, chanting, mantras and malas","question":"Give two actions Buddhists might perform during puja.","marks":2,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of: bowing/prostrating; making offerings (flowers, candles, incense, food); chanting; reciting the Three Refuges or Five Precepts; reciting mantras; using a mala; meditating; listening to a Dhamma talk; ringing a bell; circumambulating a stupa.","evidence":["Puja"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"puja"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-2-3","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Puja: devotional ritual in the home and temple, chanting, mantras and malas","question":"Give two purposes of chanting in Buddhism.","marks":2,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of: to express devotion/gratitude to the Buddha; as an aid to concentration/to calm the mind; to remember and preserve the Buddha's teaching; to make merit; to develop qualities such as compassion; for protection or blessing (paritta); to prepare for meditation.","evidence":["Chanting: devotional and concentration"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"chanting"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-2-4","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Puja: devotional ritual in the home and temple, chanting, mantras and malas","question":"Explain two contrasting ways in which Buddhists perform puja.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Many Buddhists perform puja in a temple with others, led by monks or nuns. In a Theravada vihara, for example, the community bows three times, chants the homage to the Buddha and the Three Refuges in Pali, offers flowers and food, and then listens to a Dhamma talk. This is communal and often longer.\n\n2. Other Buddhists perform puja privately at a home shrine. They might light a candle and incense, bow before a small Buddha rupa and recite a mantra on a mala before meditating, fitting this short ritual into their daily routine. In Japan, families may do this at a butsudan which also honours ancestors.","evidence":["Theravada puja","Butsudan"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"temple vs home"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-2-5","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Puja: devotional ritual in the home and temple, chanting, mantras and malas","question":"Explain two ways in which using a mala helps Buddhists in their practice.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. A mala helps Buddhists count recitations. With 108 beads, a Buddhist can recite a mantra such as 'Om mani padme hum' a set number of times without losing count, so the mind can stay on the mantra rather than on counting.\n\n2. A mala aids concentration. The physical act of moving one bead at a time through the fingers gives the mind something to anchor to, so when attention wanders the Buddhist notices and returns to the mantra. This develops mindfulness and calm.","evidence":["Mala","Mantra recitation"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"mala"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-2-6","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Puja: devotional ritual in the home and temple, chanting, mantras and malas","question":"Explain two reasons why chanting is important to Buddhists.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Chanting is a devotional practice expressing refuge and gratitude. Buddhists chant 'Buddham saranam gacchami, Dhammam saranam gacchami, Sangham saranam gacchami' ('I go for refuge to the Buddha, the Dhamma and the Sangha'), affirming their commitment and thanking the Buddha for his teaching.\n\n2. Chanting is an aid to mental concentration. The rhythm and repetition calm the mind. For example, chanting the Metta Sutta ('May all beings be happy') or the Heart Sutra focuses the mind on the teaching itself and prepares it for meditation, helping to keep distractions away.","evidence":["Three Refuges","Metta Sutta (Sutta Nipata 1.8)","Heart Sutra"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"chanting"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-2-7","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Puja: devotional ritual in the home and temple, chanting, mantras and malas","question":"Explain two ways in which Buddhists use mantras.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Tibetan Buddhists recite 'Om mani padme hum', the mantra of Avalokiteshvara, to develop compassion. They may recite it on a mala, inscribe it on prayer flags or spin it in prayer wheels, believing that its power spreads compassion to all beings. The Karandavyuha Sutra praises this mantra highly.\n\n2. Pure Land Buddhists recite the nembutsu, 'Namu Amida Butsu'. Based on Amitabha's vow in the Larger Sukhavativyuha Sutra that those who call on his name will be reborn in his Pure Land, they recite it to express faith and gratitude, both at home and in the temple, and especially at the time of death.","evidence":["Om mani padme hum (Karandavyuha Sutra)","Larger Sukhavativyuha Sutra","Nembutsu"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"mantra"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-2-8","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Puja: devotional ritual in the home and temple, chanting, mantras and malas","question":"“Puja is more important in the temple than in the home.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nPuja in a temple or vihara involves the Sangha, one of the Three Refuges. Monks, nuns or lamas lead the chanting and give Dhamma talks, so the worship is guided by experienced teachers and lay people can learn from them. This can make temple puja more meaningful and accurate.\n\nTemple puja is communal. Chanting together in a shrine hall, bowing and making offerings as a group builds a sense of community and strengthens faith, especially during festivals such as Wesak. Lay people can also make merit by giving dana directly to the monks, which is a particularly important practice in Theravada Buddhism.\n\nTemples contain special features that support devotion, such as large rupas, stupas containing relics, and the atmosphere of a dedicated sacred space. In Tibetan Buddhism, elaborate pujas with music and rituals are usually performed by monks in the gompa.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nMany Buddhists would say the home is just as important, or more so, because that is where daily practice happens. A short daily puja at a home shrine, lighting a candle, bowing and reciting the refuges, keeps the Dhamma part of everyday life, whereas many lay people can only visit a temple occasionally.\n\nThe Buddha taught that what matters is practice and intention, not location. He told his followers to be 'a lamp unto yourselves'. A sincere puja at home expresses devotion and calms the mind just as much as one in a temple. In Japan, the butsudan in the home is central to family religious life, including honouring ancestors.\n\nFor Buddhists in Britain, there may be no temple of their tradition nearby, so home puja is essential. Zen and many Western Buddhists may focus on meditation at home rather than temple ritual.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI think neither is more important overall; they serve different purposes. Temple puja connects Buddhists to the Sangha, teaching and community, while home puja keeps practice alive every day. If I had to choose, I would say home puja is more important for most lay Buddhists because it is regular and personal, but the temple remains important for guidance and festivals.","evidence":["Three Refuges","\"Be a lamp unto yourselves\"","Butsudan","Dana to the Sangha"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"temple vs home"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-2-9","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","moduleTitle":"Puja: devotional ritual in the home and temple, chanting, mantras and malas","question":"“Reciting mantras is a waste of time.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nSome people argue that repeating the same words over and over has no effect in itself. A non-religious person might see it as mere ritual or superstition, especially the idea that spinning a prayer wheel or hanging prayer flags spreads blessings. They might say this time would be better spent helping others directly.\n\nSome Buddhists, particularly in Theravada and some Western traditions, place more emphasis on meditation and ethics than on mantra recitation. The Buddha warned against attachment to rites and rituals (one of the fetters that must be broken to progress on the path), so mechanical repetition without understanding could become a distraction.\n\nMantras such as 'Om mani padme hum' are in Sanskrit, and many people reciting them may not understand their meaning, which could make the practice empty.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nMantras are an aid to concentration. Repetition, often counted on a mala, focuses the mind and calms it, much like counting breaths in meditation. Even neuroscience suggests rhythmic repetition can reduce stress. This is a practical benefit, not superstition.\n\nMantras develop qualities. Tibetan Buddhists recite 'Om mani padme hum' to cultivate compassion, often while visualising Avalokiteshvara and wishing for the welfare of all beings. Over time this shapes their attitudes and actions, which is the opposite of a waste of time.\n\nFor Pure Land Buddhists, the nembutsu is the central practice, an expression of trust in Amida's vow and gratitude for his compassion. For Nichiren Buddhists, chanting 'Nam myoho renge kyo' is believed to reveal one's Buddha-nature. For these traditions mantras are at the heart of the path.\n\nThe Buddha's warning about rites and rituals is about believing ritual alone saves you, not about practices done with understanding and intention.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI disagree with the statement. Mantra recitation can become empty if done without understanding, and that is a real danger. However, when practised with intention, mantras calm and focus the mind, develop compassion and express devotion, and for several traditions they are central to the path. So mantras are not a waste of time; their value depends on how they are used.","evidence":["Om mani padme hum","Nembutsu","Nam myoho renge kyo","Fetter of attachment to rites and rituals"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"mantra"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-3-0","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Meditation: samatha, vipassana (including zazen) and visualisation","question":"Which type of meditation focuses on developing insight?\nA) Samatha\nB) Vipassana\nC) Puja\nD) Dana","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"B) Vipassana. Vipassana means insight or 'clear seeing'. Samatha develops calm and concentration; puja is devotional worship and dana is generosity.","evidence":["Vipassana = insight"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"types of meditation"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-3-1","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Meditation: samatha, vipassana (including zazen) and visualisation","question":"Which tradition of Buddhism is zazen most associated with?\nA) Theravada\nB) Pure Land\nC) Zen\nD) Nichiren","marks":1,"command":"Multiple choice","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"C) Zen. Zazen, 'seated meditation', is the central practice of Zen Buddhism, e.g. Soto Zen's 'just sitting' (shikantaza).","evidence":["Zazen"],"examTip":"Read all four options before choosing. Only one letter is credited, and ticking more than one box scores zero.","answerTopic":"zazen"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-3-2","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Meditation: samatha, vipassana (including zazen) and visualisation","question":"Give two aims of samatha meditation.","marks":2,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of: to calm the mind; to develop concentration; to achieve tranquillity/peace; to overcome distractions/hindrances; to reach jhana (absorption); to prepare the mind for insight (vipassana); (with metta bhavana) to develop loving kindness.","evidence":["Samatha"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"samatha"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-3-3","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Meditation: samatha, vipassana (including zazen) and visualisation","question":"Give two methods of meditation used by Buddhists.","marks":2,"command":"Give","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"Any two of: mindfulness of breathing (anapanasati); counting the breath; metta bhavana (loving-kindness); vipassana/insight meditation (e.g. body scan, observing sensations); zazen/shikantaza; koan practice; visualisation of Buddhas or bodhisattvas; walking meditation; focusing on a candle or kasina; mantra meditation with a mala.","evidence":["Methods of meditation"],"examTip":"Give two separate, accurate points. One short sentence each is enough; no development is needed for 2 marks.","answerTopic":"methods"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-3-4","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Meditation: samatha, vipassana (including zazen) and visualisation","question":"Explain two contrasting methods of meditation in Buddhism.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. In samatha meditation, Buddhists focus on one object such as the breath. They sit still, follow each in-breath and out-breath, perhaps counting to ten, and gently return to the breath when the mind wanders. The aim is calm and concentration.\n\n2. In visualisation meditation, practised especially by Tibetan Buddhists, the meditator builds up a detailed mental image of a Buddha or bodhisattva such as Avalokiteshvara, often helped by a thangka. Rather than emptying the mind of images, they deliberately create one in order to develop the qualities, such as compassion, that the figure represents.","evidence":["Anapanasati","Visualisation; thangka"],"examTip":"In 'contrasting' questions, show how the methods differ, e.g. focusing on a simple object vs building a complex image.","answerTopic":"methods"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-3-5","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Meditation: samatha, vipassana (including zazen) and visualisation","question":"Explain two ways in which meditation influences the lives of Buddhists today.","marks":4,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Meditation helps Buddhists respond calmly to difficulties. By practising mindfulness of breathing, a Buddhist learns to notice anger or anxiety arising without reacting automatically, so in daily life they might pause and breathe before responding to a stressful situation.\n\n2. Meditation shapes Buddhists' routines and commitments. Many meditate every morning at a home shrine, attend weekly group sittings at a Buddhist centre, or go on retreats such as ten-day silent vipassana courses. This regular practice keeps them focused on developing wisdom and compassion.","evidence":["Mindfulness","Vipassana retreats"],"examTip":"Make two distinct points and develop each one (a detailed explanation, an example, or a link to practice). Two simple points alone cap you at 2 marks.","answerTopic":"influence"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-3-6","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Meditation: samatha, vipassana (including zazen) and visualisation","question":"Explain two aims of vipassana meditation.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Vipassana aims to see the Three Marks of Existence directly. By closely observing sensations, thoughts and feelings, a meditator sees that everything is impermanent, unsatisfactory and not-self. The Dhammapada (277) says that when one sees with wisdom that all conditioned things are impermanent, one turns away from suffering.\n\n2. Vipassana aims to develop mindfulness of the whole of experience. The Satipatthana Sutta teaches mindfulness of body, feelings, mind and mental objects, and calls this 'the direct path for the purification of beings… for the realisation of nibbana'. Insight gained in this way removes ignorance and leads towards Enlightenment.","evidence":["Dhammapada 277","Satipatthana Sutta (MN 10)"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"vipassana"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-3-7","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Meditation: samatha, vipassana (including zazen) and visualisation","question":"Explain two reasons why meditation is important to Buddhists.\nRefer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.","marks":5,"command":"Explain","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"1. Meditation is part of the Eightfold Path. Right effort, right mindfulness and right concentration form the samadhi section of the Threefold Way, so meditation is not optional but one of the three trainings that lead to nibbana. The Dhammapada (282) teaches that 'wisdom springs from meditation; without meditation wisdom wanes'.\n\n2. The Buddha achieved Enlightenment through meditation. Sitting beneath the Bodhi tree, he used mindfulness of breathing and deep concentration, as described in the Anapanasati Sutta, to gain insight into the Four Noble Truths. Buddhists meditate to follow his example and to realise the same truth for themselves.","evidence":["Dhammapada 282","Anapanasati Sutta (MN 118)"],"examTip":"Two developed points earn 4 marks; the fifth mark is for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching. Name it, quote or paraphrase it, and show how it supports your point.","answerTopic":"importance"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-3-8","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Meditation: samatha, vipassana (including zazen) and visualisation","question":"“Samatha meditation is more useful than vipassana meditation.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nSamatha meditation develops the calm, concentrated mind that all other practice depends on. Without the ability to focus, as in mindfulness of breathing, a person cannot observe their experience clearly enough for insight. It is often taught first for this reason, and the Buddha is said to have used anapanasati on the night of his Enlightenment.\n\nSamatha has immediate practical benefits. It reduces stress, anxiety and anger, and helps people concentrate in everyday life. Metta bhavana, often counted as samatha, directly develops loving kindness and weakens hatred, one of the Three Poisons. For many lay Buddhists with busy lives, these benefits are the most useful.\n\nIt is also simpler. Following the breath is something beginners can learn quickly, whereas vipassana and its subtle observation of anicca and anatta may require more guidance.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nVipassana leads to wisdom (panna), which is what actually removes ignorance and brings nibbana. Calm alone is temporary: deep concentration states (jhanas) were reached by the Buddha's early teachers, but they did not lead to Enlightenment. The Buddha's breakthrough came through insight into the Four Noble Truths and dependent arising.\n\nThe Satipatthana Sutta calls mindfulness 'the direct path… to the realisation of nibbana'. Seeing the Three Marks directly loosens craving at its root, which is a deeper benefit than temporary calm.\n\nMany teachers say the two cannot really be separated. Samatha prepares the mind for vipassana, and vipassana gives samatha its purpose, like a lamp that needs both a steady flame (calm) and brightness (insight). Zen's zazen combines calm and insight, and Tibetan visualisation also uses both.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI think vipassana is more useful for the ultimate goal of nibbana because only insight removes ignorance, as the Buddha's experience with his early teachers shows. However, samatha is more useful for beginners and for everyday wellbeing, and insight is hard to develop without it. So the best conclusion is that they are complementary: samatha is the foundation and vipassana the purpose, and neither is simply 'more useful'.","evidence":["Anapanasati Sutta","Satipatthana Sutta","Alara Kalama and Uddaka Ramaputta","Metta bhavana"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"samatha vs vipassana"},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-3-9","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","moduleTitle":"Meditation: samatha, vipassana (including zazen) and visualisation","question":"“Meditation is the most important Buddhist practice.”\nEvaluate this statement. In your answer you should:\n- refer to Buddhist teaching\n- give reasoned arguments to support this statement\n- give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view\n- reach a justified conclusion.","marks":12,"command":"Evaluate","sourcePage":null,"rubric":"","modelAnswer":"ARGUMENTS IN SUPPORT\nThe Buddha reached Enlightenment through meditation under the Bodhi tree, so meditation is directly linked to the goal of Buddhism. Three of the eight parts of the Eightfold Path (right effort, mindfulness and concentration) are about meditation, and wisdom grows from it: 'Wisdom springs from meditation' (Dhammapada 282).\n\nMeditation directly tackles the causes of suffering. Samatha calms the mind and weakens the hindrances; vipassana gives insight into anicca, anatta and dukkha, removing ignorance; metta bhavana weakens hatred. No other practice addresses the mind so directly.\n\nIn Zen, zazen is the heart of practice, and Dogen taught that sitting in zazen is itself the expression of Enlightenment. Many Western Buddhists, too, were first attracted by meditation and see it as the core of their practice.\n\nARGUMENTS FOR A DIFFERENT VIEW\nMany Buddhists would argue that ethics (sila) is the foundation. Without keeping the Five Precepts, the mind is disturbed by guilt and conflict and meditation is difficult. For many lay Buddhists in Theravada countries, the most important practices are generosity (dana), keeping the precepts and supporting the Sangha, rather than intensive meditation.\n\nPure Land Buddhists see the nembutsu, recited in faith in Amida's vow, as the essential practice. They believe ordinary people in this age cannot reach Enlightenment by their own meditative effort.\n\nMahayana Buddhists emphasise compassion. The bodhisattva practises all six perfections, and generosity comes first. A Buddhist who meditates a lot but does not act with compassion would be missing something essential.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI think meditation is arguably the most important practice for reaching Enlightenment, because insight into reality depends on it and the Buddha's own Enlightenment came through it. However, Buddhist practice is a whole: ethics provides the foundation, and different traditions prioritise devotion or compassion. So the statement is true for Zen and many meditation-focused Buddhists, but it does not reflect all Buddhists; meditation is best seen as one of three equally necessary trainings.","evidence":["Dhammapada 282","Eightfold Path: samadhi","Dogen: zazen","Nembutsu","Six perfections"],"examTip":"Argue both sides with developed reasoning, use accurate Buddhist teaching (and different traditions where relevant), then reach a justified conclusion that follows from your arguments. Spelling, punctuation, grammar and specialist terms earn up to 3 extra marks.","answerTopic":"importance"}]},"notes":[{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-1","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.1","title":"The concept of Dhamma (Dharma)","specFocus":"The concept of Dhamma (Dharma): its meanings (the Buddha's teaching, universal truth), its place among the Three Refuges, where it is found (Tipitaka and Mahayana sutras), how Buddhists use and test it (Kalama Sutta, Parable of the Raft) and why it matters today.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"WHAT DHAMMA MEANS\nDhamma (Pali) or Dharma (Sanskrit) is a word with several layers of meaning. Most importantly for GCSE, it means the teaching of the Buddha: his explanation of how things really are and of the way to end suffering. It can also mean universal truth or natural law (the way reality works whether or not a Buddha teaches it), and in Buddhist philosophy 'dhammas' are the tiny events that make up experience. Buddhists believe the Buddha did not invent the Dhamma; he discovered it, as a doctor diagnoses an illness that is already there.\n\nTHE THREE REFUGES (THREE JEWELS)\nThe Dhamma is one of the Three Jewels (tiratana): the Buddha (the teacher and example), the Dhamma (the teaching) and the Sangha (the community of monks, nuns and, in a wider sense, all practising Buddhists). Buddhists 'take refuge' in them by reciting: \"Buddham saranam gacchami, Dhammam saranam gacchami, Sangham saranam gacchami\" (\"I go for refuge to the Buddha… Dhamma… Sangha\"). Taking refuge is often seen as what makes someone a Buddhist.\n\nWHERE THE DHAMMA IS FOUND\n- Theravada Buddhists rely on the Pali Canon, the Tipitaka ('three baskets'): the Vinaya Pitaka (monastic rules), Sutta Pitaka (discourses of the Buddha, including the Dhammapada) and Abhidhamma Pitaka (philosophical analysis).\n- Mahayana Buddhists also use later sutras such as the Lotus Sutra, the Heart Sutra and the Pure Land sutras. Zen emphasises direct experience 'beyond words and letters', and Tibetan Buddhism values teachings passed from teacher (lama) to pupil.\n\nHOW BUDDHISTS TREAT THE DHAMMA\nThe Buddha encouraged people to test his teaching for themselves rather than accept it on blind faith. In the Kalama Sutta he told the Kalama people not to believe something simply because of tradition or a teacher's authority, but to accept it when they know for themselves it leads to welfare and happiness. He also compared the Dhamma to a raft (the Parable of the Raft, Majjhima Nikaya 22): it is a tool for crossing the river of suffering, not something to cling to once you have crossed. Shortly before his death he told his followers to be \"a lamp unto yourselves\" and to rely on the Dhamma as their guide.\n\nSYMBOLS\nThe Dhamma is symbolised by the Dharmachakra (Dhamma wheel). Its eight spokes represent the Eightfold Path. The Buddha's first sermon at the Deer Park in Sarnath, the Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta, is known as 'setting the wheel of Dhamma in motion'.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS TODAY\nThe Dhamma gives Buddhists a guide for ethics (the precepts), meditation and wisdom. It is studied, chanted and taught: for example, Dhamma talks at viharas such as Amaravati in Hertfordshire or teachings at Samye Ling in Scotland. Because it is about seeing reality clearly, many Buddhists see it as practical rather than as a set of beliefs about God, which is why Buddhism is often described as non-theistic.\n\nKEY TERMS\nDhamma/Dharma – the Buddha's teaching; universal truth. Tiratana – the Three Jewels. Sangha – Buddhist community. Tipitaka – Pali Canon. Sutta/sutra – a discourse or teaching. Dharmachakra – wheel of Dhamma."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-2","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.2","title":"The concept of dependent arising (paticcasamupada)","specFocus":"The concept of dependent arising (paticcasamupada): everything arises from and depends on conditions; the twelve links (nidanas); the Tibetan Wheel of Life; the link to anicca, anatta, dukkha, rebirth and the Four Noble Truths; Theravada and Mahayana (sunyata/interbeing) emphases.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"THE BASIC IDEA\nDependent arising (Pali: paticcasamupada; also translated 'dependent origination' or 'conditioned arising') is the teaching that everything arises in dependence on conditions and ceases when those conditions cease. Nothing exists independently or by itself. The Buddha summed it up simply: \"When this exists, that comes to be; with the arising of this, that arises. When this does not exist, that does not come to be; with the cessation of this, that ceases.\" (Samyutta Nikaya 12). A plant, for example, depends on a seed, soil, water, sunlight and warmth; take these away and it does not grow.\n\nWHY IT IS SO CENTRAL\nBuddhists see dependent arising as the heart of the Buddha's Enlightenment insight. In the Majjhima Nikaya (MN 28) it is said: \"Whoever sees dependent arising sees the Dhamma; whoever sees the Dhamma sees dependent arising.\" It explains:\n- Why everything is impermanent (anicca): conditions are always changing.\n- Why there is no fixed self (anatta): a person is a process depending on many causes.\n- How suffering arises and how it can end: if craving is a condition for suffering, removing craving removes suffering. This is the logic behind the Four Noble Truths.\n- How rebirth happens without a soul: one life conditions the next, like one candle lighting another.\nThe monk Sariputta became a follower after hearing a short verse from the monk Assaji: of things that arise from a cause, the Buddha has taught their cause and their ending.\n\nTHE TWELVE LINKS (NIDANAS)\nThe process of suffering and rebirth is often explained through twelve links, each the condition for the next: (1) ignorance, (2) mental formations, (3) consciousness, (4) name-and-form (mind and body), (5) the six senses, (6) contact, (7) feeling, (8) craving (tanha), (9) clinging/grasping, (10) becoming, (11) birth, (12) ageing and death. The chain can be broken, especially at the link between feeling and craving, which is why mindfulness of feelings matters.\n\nTHE TIBETAN WHEEL OF LIFE (BHAVACHAKRA)\nThis painting, common in Tibetan monasteries, shows samsara held by Yama (or Mara), a figure representing death and impermanence. At the hub are a pig, a cockerel and a snake (the Three Poisons: ignorance, greed and hatred). Around them are beings rising and falling, then six realms of rebirth (gods, demi-gods, humans, animals, hungry ghosts, hell beings), and the twelve links in pictures on the outer rim, e.g. a blind person (ignorance), a potter (mental formations), a monkey (consciousness), a person drinking (craving). Outside the wheel the Buddha points to the moon, the way out: nibbana.\n\nDIVERSITY AND CONTEMPORARY RELEVANCE\nTheravada Buddhists emphasise dependent arising as a description of how suffering and rebirth work. In Mahayana thought, dependent arising is closely linked to sunyata (emptiness): because things depend on conditions they are 'empty' of independent existence. The Vietnamese Zen teacher Thich Nhat Hanh called this 'interbeing', saying a sheet of paper contains the cloud, the rain, the tree and the logger. Many Buddhists today apply the teaching to environmental issues, seeing all life as interconnected, and to ethics, since actions always have consequences.\n\nKEY TERMS\nPaticcasamupada – dependent arising. Nidanas – the twelve links. Samsara – cycle of birth, death and rebirth. Bhavachakra – Wheel of Life. Tanha – craving. Sunyata – emptiness."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-3","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.3","title":"The Three Marks of Existence","specFocus":"The Three Marks of Existence: anicca (impermanence), anatta (no fixed self) and dukkha (unsatisfactoriness of life, suffering); how they connect; Dhammapada 277–279, the chariot analogy (Milindapanha); how Buddhists respond to them in practice.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"OVERVIEW\nThe Three Marks of Existence (tilakkhana) are three characteristics the Buddha taught are true of all conditioned things. Seeing them clearly is a key part of wisdom (panna) and of the insight that leads to nibbana. The Dhammapada (verses 277–279) states: \"All conditioned things are impermanent… all conditioned things are unsatisfactory… all things are not-self. When one sees this with wisdom, one turns away from suffering. This is the path to purity.\"\n\n1. ANICCA – IMPERMANENCE\nEverything changes. Nothing stays the same from one moment to the next: bodies age, feelings come and go, relationships, possessions, mountains and even stars change and end. This follows from dependent arising: because things depend on changing conditions, they must change too. Impermanence is not in itself bad, but suffering comes when people cling to things as if they were permanent. Buddhists reflect on anicca in meditation, and Tibetan monks create sand mandalas over many days and then sweep them away to show that all things pass. The story of Kisa Gotami, a grieving mother sent by the Buddha to find a mustard seed from a house where no one had died, teaches that death and change touch everyone.\n\n2. ANATTA – NO FIXED SELF (NOT-SELF)\nThere is no permanent, unchanging soul or self (atman) at the core of a person. What we call 'I' is a constantly changing combination of the five aggregates (skandhas): form, sensation, perception, mental formations and consciousness (see 1.4). In the Milindapanha (Questions of King Milinda), the monk Nagasena compares a person to a chariot: the chariot is not the wheels, axle or frame alone, and there is no 'chariot' apart from the parts; 'chariot' is just a convenient name. In the same way, 'Nagasena' is a name for a collection of changing parts. Anatta does not mean people do not exist; it means they do not exist as fixed, independent things. Rebirth happens without a soul, like a flame passed from one candle to another.\n\n3. DUKKHA – UNSATISFACTORINESS, SUFFERING\nLife is marked by dukkha. This includes obvious pain (illness, grief, old age), the suffering caused by change (pleasant things end) and a deeper unsatisfactoriness because nothing conditioned can give lasting satisfaction. Dukkha is the first of the Four Noble Truths (see 1.7). It is not pessimism; the Buddha taught dukkha in order to show it can be ended.\n\nHOW THE MARKS CONNECT\nBecause everything is impermanent (anicca), there is no permanent self (anatta); because people crave permanence and a fixed self, they experience dukkha. Realising the three marks loosens craving and attachment.\n\nDIVERSITY\nTheravada Buddhists use the three marks as objects of vipassana (insight) meditation. Mahayana Buddhists deepen anatta into sunyata (emptiness): all things, not just persons, lack independent existence. Some Mahayana texts on Buddha-nature speak positively of a 'true self' or Buddha-nature, but most Buddhists explain this as a potential for Enlightenment rather than a permanent soul.\n\nCONTEMPORARY EXAMPLES\nReflection on anicca helps Buddhists cope with bereavement and change; mindfulness courses in the UK draw on observing how thoughts and feelings arise and pass. Anatta encourages Buddhists to be less self-centred and more compassionate.\n\nKEY TERMS\nTilakkhana – three marks. Anicca – impermanence. Anatta – no fixed self. Dukkha – suffering/unsatisfactoriness. Atman – (Hindu) eternal self, rejected by anatta. Skandhas/khandhas – five aggregates."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-4","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.4","title":"The human personality in the Theravada and Mahayana traditions","specFocus":"The human personality in the Theravada and Mahayana traditions. Theravada: the Five Aggregates (skandhas) of form, sensation, perception, mental formations, consciousness. Mahayana: sunyata, the possibility of attaining Buddhahood and Buddha-nature.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"THE QUESTION\nWhat is a human being, if there is no permanent self (anatta)? The AQA specification asks you to know two traditions' answers: the Theravada teaching of the Five Aggregates, and the Mahayana teachings of sunyata, the possibility of attaining Buddhahood, and Buddha-nature.\n\nTHERAVADA: THE FIVE AGGREGATES (SKANDHAS / KHANDHAS)\nA person is made up of five constantly changing 'heaps' or groups:\n1. Form (rupa) – the physical body and the material world it senses.\n2. Sensation/feeling (vedana) – experiences felt as pleasant, unpleasant or neutral.\n3. Perception (sanna/samjna) – recognising and labelling what is experienced ('this is a dog').\n4. Mental formations (sankhara/samskara) – thoughts, intentions, habits, emotions and choices; this is where kamma is made.\n5. Consciousness (vinnana/vijnana) – awareness of experience through the six senses (including the mind).\nNone of these is a permanent self, and there is no self apart from them. In the Anattalakkhana Sutta (the Buddha's second sermon), the Buddha asks whether each aggregate is permanent or impermanent and whether what is impermanent can be called 'mine' or 'my self'; the monks agree it cannot. The Milindapanha's chariot analogy makes the same point. For Theravada Buddhists, seeing the aggregates clearly in vipassana meditation weakens attachment and leads towards becoming an arhat.\n\nMAHAYANA: SUNYATA (EMPTINESS)\nMahayana Buddhists extend anatta to everything: all phenomena are 'empty' (sunya) of independent, fixed existence because they arise through dependent arising. The Heart Sutra, chanted in many Mahayana temples, says: \"Form is emptiness, emptiness is form.\" The bodhisattva Avalokiteshvara sees that even the five aggregates are empty. Emptiness does not mean nothing exists; it means nothing exists by itself. The philosopher Nagarjuna (c. 2nd century CE) taught that emptiness is the 'middle way' between believing things exist permanently and believing they do not exist at all. Seeing emptiness removes the basis for clinging.\n\nMAHAYANA: BUDDHA-NATURE AND THE POSSIBILITY OF BUDDHAHOOD\nMany Mahayana traditions teach that all beings have Buddha-nature (tathagatagarbha): the seed or potential to become a Buddha. The Mahayana Mahaparinirvana Sutra teaches that all sentient beings have Buddha-nature, and the Lotus Sutra teaches that all can ultimately attain Buddhahood. Some compare Buddha-nature to gold covered in mud, or the sun hidden by clouds: it is already present but obscured by the Three Poisons. Zen teaches that Enlightenment means realising one's own true nature; Tibetan Buddhists describe the mind's natural clarity. Buddha-nature is generally understood as potential, not as an eternal soul.\n\nWHY THE DIFFERENCE MATTERS\nTheravada focuses on analysing the person to gain insight and achieve personal liberation (arhat). Mahayana's emphasis on emptiness and Buddha-nature supports the bodhisattva ideal: if all beings share Buddha-nature, all can and should be helped towards Enlightenment.\n\nCONTEMPORARY EXAMPLES\nBuddhists may use the five aggregates in meditation to notice that anger is just a passing formation, not 'who I am'. Belief in Buddha-nature encourages respect for everyone, including those in prison (e.g. Buddhist prison chaplaincy through Angulimala, the UK Buddhist prison chaplaincy organisation, named after a murderer who became an arhat).\n\nKEY TERMS\nSkandhas/khandhas – aggregates. Rupa, vedana, sanna, sankhara, vinnana – the five aggregates. Sunyata – emptiness. Tathagatagarbha – Buddha-nature, 'womb/embryo of the Buddha'. Arhat – perfected one."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-5","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.5","title":"Human destiny: the Arhat and Bodhisattva ideals, Buddhahood and the Pure Land","specFocus":"Human destiny: different ideals in the Theravada and Mahayana traditions – the Arhat ('perfected person') and Bodhisattva ideals; Buddhahood and the Pure Land.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"HUMAN DESTINY IN BUDDHISM\nBuddhists believe beings are reborn again and again in samsara according to their kamma until they achieve Enlightenment and nibbana. The traditions describe the ideal goal differently.\n\nTHERAVADA: THE ARHAT ('PERFECTED PERSON', 'WORTHY ONE')\nAn arhat (Pali: arahant) is someone who has followed the Buddha's teaching, destroyed greed, hatred and ignorance, and achieved nibbana. They will not be reborn after death. The Pali Canon describes the arhat's realisation with the words: \"Birth is ended, the holy life has been lived, what had to be done has been done, there is no more coming to any state of being.\" The Buddha himself was an arhat, but he is also a samma-sambuddha, a fully enlightened Buddha who discovered the Dhamma without a teacher. Arhats such as Sariputta, Moggallana and the nun Khema reached the goal through his teaching. Theravada Buddhists usually see this as achieved mainly by monks and nuns, through meditation and following the Vinaya, though lay people can make progress through merit-making and practice. The Dhammapada (chapter 7, 'The Arahant') describes the arhat as calm, free from fear, like a lake that is clear and undisturbed. Arhats are not selfish: they teach and help others, as the Buddha's disciples did.\n\nMAHAYANA: THE BODHISATTVA\nA bodhisattva ('Enlightenment being') is someone who aims to become a fully enlightened Buddha for the sake of all beings. Motivated by bodhicitta (the mind set on Enlightenment out of compassion), they take the bodhisattva vow, often expressed as: \"Beings are numberless; I vow to save them all.\" Shantideva's prayer says: \"For as long as space endures, and for as long as living beings remain, until then may I too abide to dispel the misery of the world\" (Bodhicaryavatara 10:55). Bodhisattvas practise the six perfections (paramitas) over many lifetimes, progressing through stages (bhumis). Some, such as Avalokiteshvara (compassion; Guanyin in China, Chenrezig in Tibet) and Manjushri (wisdom), are worshipped as celestial bodhisattvas. Tibetan Buddhists regard the Dalai Lama as an emanation of Avalokiteshvara. Mahayana Buddhists sometimes criticised the arhat goal as less complete, because it aims at personal liberation rather than Buddhahood for the sake of all.\n\nBUDDHAHOOD\nBuddhahood is the state of complete Enlightenment, with perfect wisdom and compassion. In Mahayana, it is the ultimate goal for everyone, made possible by Buddha-nature (see 1.4). There are said to have been many Buddhas, and Maitreya is expected to be the next Buddha in this world.\n\nTHE PURE LAND\nPure Land Buddhism (popular in China and Japan, e.g. Jodo Shu and Jodo Shinshu) focuses on Amitabha (Amida) Buddha. According to the Larger Sukhavativyuha Sutra, as the monk Dharmakara he made 48 vows, including the vow (the 18th) that anyone who sincerely calls on his name with faith will be reborn in his Pure Land, Sukhavati ('Land of Bliss'). There, free from distractions, beings can easily attain Enlightenment. Pure Land Buddhists recite the nembutsu: 'Namu Amida Butsu' ('I take refuge in Amida Buddha'). Honen and Shinran (12th–13th centuries) taught that in a degenerate age (mappo) people should rely on Amida's 'other-power' (tariki) rather than self-power (jiriki). Some see the Pure Land as a real place; others see it as a symbol of a purified mind.\n\nKEY TERMS\nArhat – perfected one. Bodhisattva – being who seeks Buddhahood for all. Bodhicitta – compassionate aspiration for Enlightenment. Paramitas – perfections. Sukhavati – Amitabha's Pure Land. Nembutsu – recitation of Amida's name."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-6","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.6","title":"The Buddha's life and its significance","specFocus":"The Buddha's life and its significance: the birth of the Buddha and his life of luxury; the Four Sights: illness, old age, death, holy man (Jataka 075); the Buddha's ascetic life; the Buddha's Enlightenment.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"WHY THE BUDDHA'S LIFE MATTERS\nSiddhattha Gotama (Sanskrit: Siddhartha Gautama), probably living in the 5th century BCE in northern India/Nepal, is not worshipped as a god. Buddhists see his life as an example showing that a human being can overcome suffering, and each stage teaches part of the Dhamma. Accounts come from the Pali Canon, the Jataka commentaries and later works such as Ashvaghosha's Buddhacarita.\n\n1. BIRTH AND LIFE OF LUXURY\nQueen Maya dreamed that a white elephant entered her side, a sign that she would bear a great being. Siddhattha was born in the Lumbini grove; traditional accounts say he was born from her side, took seven steps, and lotus flowers sprang up beneath his feet. The sage Asita predicted he would become either a great ruler or a great spiritual teacher. His mother died seven days after the birth and he was raised by her sister Mahapajapati. His father, King Suddhodana of the Sakya clan, wanted him to become a king, so he surrounded him with luxury in three palaces and kept suffering from his sight. Siddhattha married Yasodhara, and they had a son, Rahula. Significance: even wealth, pleasure and family could not give lasting satisfaction (dukkha).\n\n2. THE FOUR SIGHTS\nLeaving the palace with his charioteer Channa, Siddhattha saw an old man, a sick man, a corpse and a holy man (a wandering ascetic). (The AQA specification cites Jataka 075 for this.) Channa explained that ageing, illness and death come to everyone. The holy man, calm despite having nothing, suggested a way of seeking an answer to suffering. Significance: the first three sights show the reality of dukkha and anicca; the fourth shows that a spiritual path is possible. Buddhists today reflect on these as reminders of impermanence.\n\n3. THE ASCETIC LIFE\nAt 29 Siddhattha left the palace at night on his horse Kanthaka (the Great Renunciation), cut off his hair and put on simple robes. He studied meditation with teachers such as Alara Kalama and Uddaka Ramaputta, reaching deep states but not liberation. He then joined five ascetics and practised extreme self-denial for about six years, eating so little that he became skeletal. He realised that extreme asceticism did not lead to Enlightenment, accepted a bowl of milk-rice from a young woman, Sujata, and the five ascetics left him in disgust. Significance: the Middle Way between luxury and extreme self-denial. It is often illustrated by the lute string: too tight it snaps, too loose it will not play.\n\n4. THE ENLIGHTENMENT\nAt about 35, at Bodh Gaya, he sat beneath a pipal tree (the Bodhi tree) and vowed not to rise until he had found the truth. Mara, the personification of temptation and death, attacked him with armies, storms and his beautiful daughters, and challenged his right to sit there. Siddhattha touched the earth to call it as witness (the bhumisparsha mudra, seen on many Buddha rupas), and Mara fled. During the three watches of the night he gained knowledge of his past lives, saw how beings are reborn according to their kamma, and understood the Four Noble Truths and the end of craving. He became the Buddha, 'the awakened one'. Significance: Enlightenment is possible for a human being through effort, meditation and wisdom. After being encouraged (in tradition by the god Brahma Sahampati) to teach, he gave his first sermon to the five ascetics at Sarnath.\n\nCONTEMPORARY RELEVANCE\nWesak celebrates the Buddha's birth, Enlightenment and (in Theravada) death. Pilgrims visit Lumbini, Bodh Gaya, Sarnath and Kushinagar. Rupas show his life through mudras: meditation, earth-touching and teaching gestures.\n\nKEY TERMS\nSiddhattha/Siddhartha – the Buddha's personal name. Jataka – stories of the Buddha's lives. Ascetic – someone practising severe self-discipline. Middle Way – avoiding extremes. Mara – personification of temptation. Bodhi – awakening."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-7","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.7","title":"The Four Noble Truths: suffering (dukkha) and its different types","specFocus":"The Four Noble Truths – the first truth: suffering (dukkha), including different types of suffering (ordinary suffering, suffering of change, suffering of conditioned existence); the Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta; the doctor analogy; whether the teaching is pessimistic.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"THE FOUR NOBLE TRUTHS: OVERVIEW\nThe Four Noble Truths (cattari ariya saccani) were the subject of the Buddha's first sermon at the Deer Park, Sarnath, to the five ascetics: the Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta ('Setting in Motion the Wheel of Dhamma'). They are often compared to a doctor's method:\n1. Dukkha – the illness (diagnosis): suffering exists.\n2. Samudaya – the cause of the illness: craving (tanha).\n3. Nirodha – the prognosis: suffering can end (nibbana).\n4. Magga – the cure/prescription: the Eightfold Path.\nThe Buddha said each truth has a task: dukkha is to be understood, its cause abandoned, its cessation realised and the path developed.\n\nTHE FIRST NOBLE TRUTH: DUKKHA\nDukkha is often translated as 'suffering', but it also means unsatisfactoriness, stress or imperfection, like a wheel that does not run smoothly on its axle. In the first sermon the Buddha explained: \"Birth is dukkha, ageing is dukkha, illness is dukkha, death is dukkha; sorrow, lamentation, pain, grief and despair are dukkha; union with what is disliked is dukkha; separation from what is liked is dukkha; not getting what one wants is dukkha. In brief, the five aggregates subject to clinging are dukkha.\"\n\nDIFFERENT TYPES OF SUFFERING\nBuddhist teaching identifies three types:\n1. Dukkha-dukkha – ordinary suffering: physical and mental pain such as injury, illness, grief, fear or loneliness. Everyone recognises this.\n2. Viparinama-dukkha – the suffering of change: pleasant experiences are impermanent, so even happiness carries dissatisfaction because it ends (a holiday finishing, a friendship ending, youth fading). This links directly to anicca.\n3. Sankhara-dukkha – suffering of conditioned existence (existential suffering): a subtle, constant unsatisfactoriness because everything, including our own personality (the five aggregates), is conditioned, changing and lacks a fixed self. Nothing in samsara can give perfect, lasting satisfaction.\n\nIS IT PESSIMISTIC?\nBuddhists argue that dukkha is realism, not pessimism. The Buddha did not say life has no happiness; he said conditioned happiness cannot last. Like a good doctor, he named the problem in order to cure it, and the third and fourth truths show it can be overcome. The story of Kisa Gotami illustrates this: she came to the Buddha with her dead child and was asked to bring a mustard seed from a house where no one had died. Finding none, she understood that death and loss are universal, and she became a nun.\n\nDIVERSITY\nAll Buddhist traditions accept the Four Noble Truths. Theravada Buddhists often emphasise understanding dukkha through vipassana meditation. Mahayana Buddhists add that bodhisattvas respond to the dukkha of all beings with compassion; the Heart Sutra even says that from the standpoint of emptiness there is 'no suffering, no origin, no cessation, no path', meaning the truths too are empty of fixed existence.\n\nCONTEMPORARY EXAMPLES\nRecognising dukkha leads Buddhists to compassion: e.g. hospice work, mental-health support using mindfulness-based approaches, and the work of engaged Buddhist groups helping refugees and the homeless.\n\nKEY TERMS\nDukkha – suffering/unsatisfactoriness. Dukkha-dukkha – ordinary suffering. Viparinama-dukkha – suffering of change. Sankhara-dukkha – suffering of conditioned existence. Sarnath – site of the first sermon."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-8","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.8","title":"The Four Noble Truths: the causes of suffering (samudaya) and the Three Poisons","specFocus":"The Four Noble Truths – the second truth: the causes of suffering (samudaya); the Three Poisons: ignorance, greed and hate; craving (tanha) and its three kinds; the Fire Sermon; antidotes in practice.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"THE SECOND NOBLE TRUTH: SAMUDAYA\nSamudaya means 'arising' or 'origin'. The second Noble Truth teaches that suffering has a cause, and that cause is tanha (craving, literally 'thirst'). In the first sermon the Buddha described it as \"this craving which leads to renewed existence, accompanied by delight and lust, seeking delight here and there\". Because of dependent arising, if the cause can be removed, suffering can be removed.\n\nTHREE KINDS OF CRAVING (TANHA)\n1. Kama-tanha – craving for sense pleasures: food, possessions, sex, entertainment, pleasant experiences.\n2. Bhava-tanha – craving for existence or becoming: wanting to be someone, to have a fixed identity, status or fame, to live for ever.\n3. Vibhava-tanha – craving for non-existence: wanting to get rid of unpleasant things, to escape or not to exist (e.g. wishing a situation or person would disappear).\nCraving is bound up with clinging (upadana): holding on to things that are impermanent and treating them as 'mine'.\n\nTHE THREE POISONS (THREE FIRES)\nThe AQA specification names the Three Poisons (Three Fires or Three Unwholesome Roots) as causes of suffering:\n- Ignorance (moha/avijja) – not seeing reality as it is: not understanding anicca, anatta and dukkha. It is the root of the other two, and the first link of dependent arising. Symbol: the pig.\n- Greed (lobha/raga) – attachment, desire, wanting more. Symbol: the cockerel.\n- Hatred (dosa) – aversion, anger, ill will. Symbol: the snake.\nOn the Tibetan Wheel of Life they appear at the hub, each biting the tail of the next, showing they fuel one another and keep the wheel of samsara turning.\n\nSCRIPTURE\n- The Fire Sermon (Adittapariyaya Sutta, Samyutta Nikaya 35.28): \"Everything is burning… burning with the fire of passion, with the fire of aversion, with the fire of delusion.\" Nibbana literally means 'blowing out' these fires.\n- Dhammapada 251: \"There is no fire like lust, no grip like hatred, no net like delusion, no river like craving.\"\n- Dhammapada 216: \"From craving springs grief, from craving springs fear; for one who is wholly free from craving there is no grief, much less fear.\"\n\nTHE ANTIDOTES\nEach poison has an opposite wholesome root: generosity (dana) counters greed; loving kindness (metta) counters hatred; wisdom (panna) counters ignorance. So Buddhist practice targets the causes of suffering directly: giving to the Sangha, metta bhavana meditation and study of the Dhamma.\n\nDIVERSITY AND CONTEMPORARY EXAMPLES\nAll traditions accept the second truth. Theravada stresses removing craving through mindfulness and insight. Mahayana teachers note that bodhisattvas still have desire to help others, but this is wholesome aspiration (chanda), not selfish craving. Many Buddhists today apply the teaching to consumerism and advertising, which encourage kama-tanha; to social media, which can fuel bhava-tanha (craving for status); and to environmental damage driven by greed. Some Western Buddhist groups run 'mindful consumption' or 'buy nothing' initiatives.\n\nKEY TERMS\nSamudaya – origin of suffering. Tanha – craving. Upadana – clinging. Moha/avijja – ignorance. Lobha/raga – greed. Dosa – hatred. Dana – generosity."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-9","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.9","title":"The Four Noble Truths: the end of craving (nirodha), nibbana and Enlightenment","specFocus":"The Four Noble Truths – the third truth: the end of craving (tanha) (nirodha); interpretations of nibbana (nirvana) and Enlightenment: in this life and parinibbana, annihilation or not, Theravada, Mahayana (non-duality), Zen and Pure Land understandings.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"THE THIRD NOBLE TRUTH: NIRODHA\nNirodha means 'cessation'. The third Noble Truth teaches that suffering can end, because its cause (craving, tanha) can end. In the first sermon the Buddha described it as \"the complete fading away and cessation of that very craving, giving it up, letting it go, being free from it\". The state in which craving and the Three Poisons have ended is called nibbana (Pali) or nirvana (Sanskrit). This is the most hopeful of the truths: like a doctor's prognosis, it says the illness is curable.\n\nWHAT DOES NIBBANA MEAN?\nNibbana literally means 'blowing out' or 'extinguishing', as a flame goes out. What is blown out is the fire of greed, hatred and ignorance (see the Fire Sermon). It is not a place like heaven and it is not God. It is described as:\n- The end of suffering and of rebirth.\n- \"The highest happiness\" (Dhammapada 204: \"Nibbana is the highest bliss\").\n- Unconditioned: \"There is, monks, an unborn, unbecome, unmade, unconditioned. If there were not, there would be no escape from the born, become, made and conditioned\" (Udana 8.3).\n- Peace, freedom, the 'further shore', the deathless.\nMuch of the language is negative ('not this, not that') because nibbana is beyond ordinary experience and words.\n\nNIBBANA IN THIS LIFE AND AT DEATH\n- Nibbana in life (sometimes called 'with remainder'): the Buddha achieved it at Enlightenment aged 35. An enlightened person still has a body and feels physical pain, but is free from craving and mental suffering.\n- Parinibbana (final nibbana, 'without remainder'): at death, an enlightened being is not reborn. The Buddha's parinibbana at Kushinagar aged about 80 is remembered on Parinirvana Day.\n\nINTERPRETATIONS\n- Does nibbana mean annihilation? When asked where an enlightened person goes after death, the Buddha refused to say they exist, do not exist, both or neither. In the Aggi-Vacchagotta Sutta (MN 72) he compared this to asking where a fire has gone when it goes out: the question does not apply. He rejected both eternalism and annihilationism.\n- Theravada: nibbana is the goal of the arhat, reached by following the Eightfold Path, usually gradually over many lifetimes.\n- Mahayana: many teach that nirvana and samsara are not ultimately separate; Nagarjuna said there is no difference between them when seen with the wisdom of emptiness. The bodhisattva achieves a 'non-abiding nirvana', staying engaged with the world to help others.\n- Zen: Enlightenment (satori or kensho, 'seeing one's nature') can be experienced suddenly, as an awakening to one's Buddha-nature here and now.\n- Pure Land: faith in Amida leads to rebirth in the Pure Land, where Enlightenment is then attained.\n\nENLIGHTENMENT\n'Enlightenment' (bodhi, 'awakening') is the insight that brings nibbana: seeing the truth of the Four Noble Truths, the Three Marks and dependent arising directly, not just believing them.\n\nCONTEMPORARY RELEVANCE\nBelief that suffering can end gives Buddhists hope and motivation to practise. Even partial ending of craving brings peace, so many describe 'tastes' of nibbana in moments of deep calm in meditation.\n\nKEY TERMS\nNirodha – cessation. Nibbana/nirvana – 'blowing out'; the end of suffering. Parinibbana – final nibbana at death. Bodhi – awakening. Satori/kensho – Zen awakening. Unconditioned – not dependent on causes."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper1-1-10","paper":"Beliefs","module":"1.10","title":"The Four Noble Truths: the Eightfold Path (magga) and the Threefold Way","specFocus":"The Four Noble Truths – the fourth truth: the Eightfold Path (magga) to nibbana/nirvana; the path as the Threefold Way: ethics (sila), meditation (samadhi) and wisdom (panna). Dhammapada 190–191.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"THE FOURTH NOBLE TRUTH: MAGGA\nMagga means 'path'. The fourth Noble Truth is that there is a way to end suffering: the Noble Eightfold Path (ariya atthangika magga). It is the 'Middle Way' between indulgence and harsh self-denial that the Buddha discovered after his ascetic years. The Dhammapada links the refuges and the Four Noble Truths: \"He who has gone for refuge to the Buddha, the Dhamma and the Sangha, sees with right wisdom the Four Noble Truths: suffering, the arising of suffering, the overcoming of suffering, and the Noble Eightfold Path leading to the cessation of suffering\" (Dhammapada 190–191).\n\nTHE EIGHT ELEMENTS\nEach element begins with samma, meaning 'right', 'complete' or 'perfect'. They are grouped into the Threefold Way (three trainings):\n\nWISDOM (PANNA)\n1. Right understanding/view (samma ditthi) – seeing reality as it is: the Four Noble Truths, the Three Marks, kamma.\n2. Right intention/thought (samma sankappa) – thoughts of renunciation, goodwill and harmlessness rather than greed and ill will.\n\nETHICS (SILA)\n3. Right speech (samma vaca) – no lying, harsh words, gossip or divisive speech; speaking truthfully and kindly.\n4. Right action (samma kammanta) – not harming, not stealing, not misusing the senses; linked to the Five Precepts.\n5. Right livelihood (samma ajiva) – earning a living without causing harm. The Buddha listed trades to avoid: dealing in weapons, living beings (e.g. slave trade, raising animals for slaughter), meat, intoxicants and poisons.\n\nMEDITATION (SAMADHI)\n6. Right effort (samma vayama) – preventing and abandoning unwholesome states, developing and maintaining wholesome ones.\n7. Right mindfulness (samma sati) – awareness of body, feelings, mind and mental objects in the present moment (Satipatthana Sutta).\n8. Right concentration (samma samadhi) – the focused, calm mind developed in meditation (e.g. the jhanas).\n\nHOW THE PATH WORKS\nThe path is not a ladder of steps done in order. It is symbolised by the eight spokes of the Dhamma wheel (dharmachakra): each spoke is needed for the wheel to turn. The three trainings support one another: ethics calms the mind for meditation; meditation brings insight (wisdom); wisdom leads naturally to ethical behaviour. Traditionally, sila comes first as the foundation, though wisdom appears first in the list because right view gives direction.\n\nDIVERSITY\nAll traditions follow the Eightfold Path. Theravada monks follow it within the discipline of the Vinaya (227 rules for bhikkhus). Mahayana Buddhists often express the path through the six perfections of the bodhisattva (see 2.8), which overlap with the threefold way. Zen emphasises meditation (zazen) as the heart of practice; Tibetan Buddhists combine study, ethics and visualisation meditation. In the Triratna Buddhist Community (UK-founded), right livelihood inspired 'team-based right livelihood' businesses: small ethical businesses run cooperatively by Buddhists.\n\nCONTEMPORARY EXAMPLES\nBuddhists might refuse jobs in arms manufacturing or the alcohol trade (right livelihood), avoid online bullying and gossip (right speech), and practise mindfulness daily (right mindfulness). Many people have taken up mindfulness-based programmes in the NHS and schools, derived partly from samma sati.\n\nKEY TERMS\nMagga – path. Samma – right/complete. Sila – ethics. Samadhi – meditation/concentration. Panna – wisdom. Threefold Way – the three trainings. Dharmachakra – Dhamma wheel. Middle Way – avoiding extremes."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-1","paper":"Practices","module":"2.1","title":"Buddhist places of worship: temples, shrines, viharas and gompas, Buddha rupa, artefacts and offerings","specFocus":"The nature, use and importance of Buddhist places of worship, including temples, shrines, monasteries (viharas), halls for meditation or learning (gompas), and their key features, including Buddha rupa, artefacts and offerings.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"TYPES OF BUDDHIST PLACE OF WORSHIP\n- Temples: buildings for worship and teaching, often with a shrine room, meditation hall and sometimes a stupa. Styles vary: Thai temples (wats) with tiered roofs, Japanese Zen temples with gardens, Chinese temples with images of bodhisattvas such as Guanyin.\n- Viharas (monasteries): where monks (bhikkhus) and nuns (bhikkhunis) live, meditate and study; lay people visit to make offerings, hear Dhamma talks and join festivals. Examples in Britain: Amaravati (Hertfordshire) and Chithurst (West Sussex) in the Thai Forest tradition; the London Buddhist Vihara (Sri Lankan).\n- Gompas: Tibetan halls or monasteries for meditation and learning, e.g. Kagyu Samye Ling in Dumfriesshire, founded in 1967 as the first Tibetan Buddhist centre in the West.\n- Halls for meditation or learning: e.g. a Zen zendo, or city Buddhist centres such as those of the Triratna Buddhist Community.\n- Shrines: a focus for devotion, either in a temple or at home: a shelf or small room with a Buddha rupa and offerings.\n- Stupas (and pagodas): dome-shaped or tower monuments, often containing relics. Buddhists walk around them clockwise (circumambulation) as an act of devotion.\n\nKEY FEATURES\nThe Buddha rupa (image of the Buddha) is the main focus of the shrine. It is not worshipped as a god but reminds Buddhists of the Buddha's qualities and their own potential. Features include:\n- The ushnisha (bump on the head) – wisdom/Enlightenment.\n- The urna (dot between the eyebrows) – spiritual insight, a 'third eye'.\n- Long ear lobes – his former life of luxury (heavy earrings), and that he listens to the world's suffering.\n- Half-closed eyes – meditation, looking inward.\n- Mudras (hand gestures): dhyana (meditation), bhumisparsha (earth-touching, from his Enlightenment), dharmachakra (teaching), abhaya (fearlessness/protection).\nRupas of bodhisattvas (e.g. Avalokiteshvara with many arms) are common in Mahayana temples.\n\nARTEFACTS AND OFFERINGS\n- Flowers – beauty that fades: a reminder of anicca.\n- Candles or lamps – the light of the Buddha's wisdom dispelling the darkness of ignorance.\n- Incense – the 'fragrance' of the Dhamma and of good conduct spreading in all directions; also purifies the space.\n- Water bowls – especially in Tibetan shrines, often seven bowls representing offerings to an honoured guest (water to drink and wash, flowers, incense, light, perfume, food); they express generosity and non-attachment.\n- Food – offered to the Buddha and given to monks (dana), making merit.\n- Others: prayer wheels and prayer flags (Tibetan), bells, singing bowls, malas, thangkas (painted scrolls), mandalas, scriptures.\n\nETIQUETTE AND IMPORTANCE\nVisitors remove shoes, sit lower than the rupa and avoid pointing their feet at it; many bow three times (to the Buddha, Dhamma and Sangha). Places of worship matter because they provide a calm place for meditation and puja, teaching from the Sangha, a focus for community and festivals, and opportunities to make merit. However, worship at a home shrine is just as valid for many Buddhists, and some, especially in Western convert communities, rarely visit a temple.\n\nKEY TERMS\nVihara – monastery. Gompa – Tibetan hall/monastery. Rupa – image (form) of the Buddha. Mudra – symbolic hand gesture. Stupa – relic monument. Dana – generosity/giving. Thangka – Tibetan painted scroll."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-2","paper":"Practices","module":"2.2","title":"Puja: devotional ritual in the home and temple, chanting, mantras and malas","specFocus":"Puja, the significance and role of puja/devotional ritual in the home and in the temple, including chanting, both as a devotional practice and as an aid to mental concentration, mantra recitation, use of malas.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"WHAT IS PUJA?\nPuja means 'worship' or 'devotional ritual'. In Buddhism it is not worship of a creator God but an expression of respect, gratitude and devotion towards the Buddha, the Dhamma and the Sangha, and a way of developing wholesome qualities. Puja may include bowing, making offerings (flowers, light, incense, food), chanting, reciting the Three Refuges and Five Precepts, mantra recitation and meditation.\n\nPUJA IN THE TEMPLE\nIn a temple or vihara, puja is often led by monks, nuns or lamas. In a typical Theravada puja the community bows three times before the rupa, chants the homage \"Namo tassa bhagavato arahato samma-sambuddhassa\" (\"Homage to the Blessed One, the Worthy One, the Fully Enlightened One\"), takes the Three Refuges and the Five Precepts in Pali, makes offerings, listens to a Dhamma talk and meditates. Monks may chant paritta (protective verses) such as the Metta Sutta and bless the congregation. Tibetan pujas can involve long chanting with drums, horns and cymbals, and full-length prostrations. Communal puja builds the Sangha and is central at festivals.\n\nPUJA IN THE HOME\nMany Buddhists have a home shrine with a Buddha rupa (or bodhisattva image, or a picture of a teacher), candles, incense and fresh flowers. Daily puja may be brief: lighting a candle, bowing, reciting the refuges and meditating. It brings Buddhist practice into ordinary life and allows the whole family to take part. In Japan, many homes have a butsudan (household altar), often also honouring ancestors.\n\nCHANTING\nChanting has two main purposes named in the AQA specification:\n1. As a devotional practice – expressing faith, gratitude and refuge; reciting words of the Buddha keeps the Dhamma alive and makes merit.\n2. As an aid to mental concentration – the rhythm and repetition calm and focus the mind, preparing for meditation and helping to keep out distracting thoughts.\nExamples include chanting suttas, the Heart Sutra in Zen and other Mahayana temples, and the nembutsu in Pure Land.\n\nMANTRA RECITATION\nA mantra is a sacred sound, word or phrase repeated many times. The most famous is \"Om mani padme hum\", associated with Avalokiteshvara, the bodhisattva of compassion, especially in Tibetan Buddhism; it is said to cultivate compassion. Pure Land Buddhists recite \"Namu Amida Butsu\" (I take refuge in Amida Buddha). Nichiren Buddhists (e.g. Soka Gakkai) chant \"Nam myoho renge kyo\", honouring the Lotus Sutra. Tibetan Buddhists also write mantras on prayer flags and inside prayer wheels; turning the wheel is believed to have the same effect as reciting the mantra.\n\nMALAS\nA mala is a string of prayer beads, usually 108 beads (a number often linked to the 108 mental defilements or kinds of feeling). Buddhists move one bead at a time between their fingers while reciting a mantra or the refuges, which helps them count repetitions and keeps attention focused. Wrist malas with 27 or 21 beads are also used.\n\nIMPORTANCE AND DIVERSITY\nPuja helps Buddhists develop devotion (saddha), gratitude, humility and concentration, and makes merit. Theravada puja tends to focus on the historical Buddha; Mahayana and Tibetan puja often include bodhisattvas and visualisation; Zen places less emphasis on devotional ritual and more on zazen, though chanting is still used. Some Western Buddhists practise little formal puja.\n\nKEY TERMS\nPuja – devotional worship. Mantra – sacred sound repeated. Mala – prayer beads. Paritta – protective chanting. Butsudan – Japanese home altar. Saddha – faith/confidence."},{"id":"aqa-rs-a-buddhism-paper2-2-3","paper":"Practices","module":"2.3","title":"Meditation: samatha, vipassana (including zazen) and visualisation","specFocus":"Meditation, the different aims, significance and methods of meditation: Samatha (concentration and tranquillity) including mindfulness of breathing; Vipassana (insight) including zazen; the visualisation of Buddhas and Bodhisattvas.","sourcePages":"Written to match the published AQA GCSE specification.","summary":"WHY MEDITATE?\nMeditation (bhavana, 'mental cultivation' or 'development') is part of the samadhi section of the Eightfold Path (right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration). The Buddha reached Enlightenment through meditation, and Buddhists meditate to calm the mind, develop concentration and insight, reduce the Three Poisons and move towards nibbana. The Dhammapada says: \"Wisdom springs from meditation; without meditation wisdom wanes\" (verse 282).\n\n1. SAMATHA – CONCENTRATION AND TRANQUILLITY\nSamatha meditation aims to calm and focus the mind on a single object, overcoming distractions such as the five hindrances (sensual desire, ill will, sloth, restlessness and doubt). As concentration deepens, the meditator may reach states of deep absorption (jhanas).\n- Mindfulness of breathing (anapanasati) is the most common method. The meditator sits upright, often cross-legged, and focuses attention on the breath, perhaps counting breaths from one to ten. When the mind wanders, they gently bring it back. The Anapanasati Sutta (MN 118) teaches this method, and the Buddha is said to have used it on the night of his Enlightenment.\n- Other samatha objects include a candle flame, a coloured disc (kasina) and the metta bhavana (loving-kindness meditation), in which goodwill is developed towards oneself, a friend, a neutral person, a difficult person and all beings.\nAim: a calm, still, concentrated mind, like a still lake.\n\n2. VIPASSANA – INSIGHT\nVipassana means 'clear seeing'. It uses the calm mind to observe experience closely and see the Three Marks of Existence directly: that all sensations, thoughts and feelings are impermanent (anicca), unsatisfactory (dukkha) and not-self (anatta). The Satipatthana Sutta (MN 10) teaches the four foundations of mindfulness: body, feelings, mind and mental objects. Vipassana is popular in Theravada, and S. N. Goenka's ten-day silent courses (including at Dhamma Dipa in Herefordshire) have spread it worldwide.\nZazen ('seated meditation') in Zen Buddhism is included by the AQA specification here. Soto Zen practises shikantaza ('just sitting'), advocated by Dogen: sitting upright, facing a wall, letting thoughts come and go without attachment, to realise one's Buddha-nature. Rinzai Zen also uses koans (puzzling questions such as 'What is the sound of one hand clapping?') to break through ordinary thinking.\nAim: wisdom (panna) and Enlightenment.\n\n3. VISUALISATION OF BUDDHAS AND BODHISATTVAS\nPractised especially in Tibetan and other Mahayana traditions. The meditator builds up a detailed mental image of a Buddha or bodhisattva, such as Avalokiteshvara or Tara, often helped by a thangka (painted scroll) or rupa, sometimes while reciting their mantra. The aim is to develop the qualities they represent (e.g. compassion) and, in advanced Tibetan practice (deity yoga), to identify with the enlightened being and recognise that one's own mind has the same Buddha-nature, before dissolving the image into emptiness. Some Pure Land texts (the Contemplation Sutra) teach visualising Amitabha and his Pure Land.\n\nDIVERSITY AND CONTEMPORARY PRACTICE\nTheravada emphasises samatha and vipassana; Zen emphasises zazen; Tibetan Buddhism combines all three; Pure Land emphasises the nembutsu. In Britain, mindfulness-based cognitive therapy (MBCT), recommended within the NHS for recurrent depression, draws on Buddhist mindfulness techniques. Many Buddhists attend meditation retreats.\n\nKEY TERMS\nBhavana – mental cultivation/meditation. Samatha – calm. Anapanasati – mindfulness of breathing. Jhana – meditative absorption. Vipassana – insight. Zazen – seated meditation. Shikantaza – 'just sitting'. Koan – Zen riddle. Thangka – painted scroll."}],"rubrics":{"Beliefs":{"1":"1-mark multiple choice (AO1): one correct answer from four options (A–D). 1 mark for the correct letter only.","12":"12-mark 'Evaluate this statement' (AO2), plus up to 3 SPaG marks. Answers should refer to Buddhist teaching, may refer to non-religious arguments, and must reach a justified conclusion. Level 4 (10–12): well-argued response with reasoned consideration of different points of view; logical chains of reasoning leading to judgements supported by knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information. Level 3 (7–9): reasoned consideration of different points of view, with logical chains of reasoning drawing on knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information. Level 2 (4–6): reasoned consideration of a point of view, or recognition of different points of view each supported by relevant reasons or evidence. Level 1 (1–3): point of view with reason(s) stated in support. (Paraphrase of AQA's generic levels; see the official specification and mark schemes for exact wording.)","2":"2-mark 'Give two…' / 'Name two…' (AO1): 1 mark for each of two correct, relevant points. No development is required.","4":"4-mark 'Explain two ways…' / 'Explain two contrasting beliefs…' (AO1): for each of two points, 1 mark for a simple explanation of a relevant and accurate belief, or 2 marks for a detailed explanation. Maximum 4.","5":"5-mark 'Explain two… Refer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.' (AO1): for each of two points, 1 mark for a simple explanation or 2 marks for a detailed explanation (maximum 4), plus 1 mark for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching."},"Practices":{"1":"1-mark multiple choice (AO1): one correct answer from four options (A–D). 1 mark for the correct letter only.","12":"12-mark 'Evaluate this statement' (AO2), plus up to 3 SPaG marks. Answers should refer to Buddhist teaching, may refer to non-religious arguments, and must reach a justified conclusion. Level 4 (10–12): well-argued response with reasoned consideration of different points of view; logical chains of reasoning leading to judgements supported by knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information. Level 3 (7–9): reasoned consideration of different points of view, with logical chains of reasoning drawing on knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information. Level 2 (4–6): reasoned consideration of a point of view, or recognition of different points of view each supported by relevant reasons or evidence. Level 1 (1–3): point of view with reason(s) stated in support. (Paraphrase of AQA's generic levels; see the official specification and mark schemes for exact wording.)","2":"2-mark 'Give two…' / 'Name two…' (AO1): 1 mark for each of two correct, relevant points. No development is required.","4":"4-mark 'Explain two ways in which belief about … influences Buddhists today' / 'Explain two contrasting ways…' (AO1): for each of two points, 1 mark for a simple explanation of a relevant and accurate influence or practice, or 2 marks for a detailed explanation. Maximum 4.","5":"5-mark 'Explain two… Refer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer.' (AO1): for each of two points, 1 mark for a simple explanation or 2 marks for a detailed explanation (maximum 4), plus 1 mark for a relevant, accurate reference to sacred writing or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching."}},"markingSchema":{"dsl":"SCHEME AQA Religious Studies adaptable guidance\nCOMMAND ALL\nMARKS AUTO\nREQUIRE use accurate subject knowledge and answer the command word\nREQUIRE support religious or non-religious views where the question asks for them\nNEVER invent quotations, sources or official level descriptors\nFEEDBACK be specific, supportive and honest about missing content\n\nCOMMAND Explain\nMARKS AUTO\nAWARD 1 one accurate relevant point\nAWARD 1 a clear linked explanation\n\nCOMMAND Evaluate\nMARKS AUTO\nBAND 9-12 well-argued evaluation with a justified conclusion\nBAND 5-8 relevant discussion with some evaluation\nBAND 1-4 limited points with little evaluation","id":"aqa-rs-generic","references":[{"title":"AQA Religious Studies 8062 specification","url":"https://www.aqa.org.uk/subjects/religious-studies/gcse/religious-studies-8062/specification"},{"title":"AQA scheme of assessment","url":"https://www.aqa.org.uk/subjects/religious-studies/gcse/religious-studies-8062/specification/scheme-of-assessment"}],"sourceNotes":"AQA GCSE RS A uses 1/1/4/6/12 question structures and a separate SPaG overlay. Check the current official specification and mark scheme before relying on a mark estimate.","status":"adaptable","title":"AQA Religious Studies · check and adapt","version":1},"moduleCounts":{"paper1":10,"paper2":3},"meta":{"modulesTotal":13,"paper1QuestionCount":100,"paper2QuestionCount":30,"provenance":"Original practice questions, revision notes and indicative model answers written to the published AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062) specification (topic list, question styles and mark tariffs). This is not AQA past-paper material and it does not reproduce AQA mark schemes, textbooks or revision guides. Model answers are indicative exemplars only, not official mark schemes. Short scripture quotations (Dhammapada, Pali Canon, Mahayana sutras) are paraphrased or quoted briefly for study purposes.","qualification":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062), Component 1: The study of religions, Buddhism (3.1.1.1 Key beliefs, 3.1.1.2 Practices)","questionTotal":130,"title":"AQA GCSE Religious Studies A: Buddhism"}});
